window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert-math-straight-lines","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Straight Lines · Practice","chapter":"Straight Lines","subject":"Maths","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":1,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line meets the x-axis at A and the y-axis at B, and the point (3, 2) is the mid-point of the segment AB.\nStatement I: The equation of the line is $2x + 3y = 12$.\nStatement II: The area of the triangle formed by this line and the coordinate axes is 6 square units.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let A = (a, 0) and B = (0, b). The mid-point is $\\left(\\frac{a}{2}, \\frac{b}{2}\\right) = (3, 2)$, so a = 6 and b = 4.\nStatement I is correct: the line is $\\frac{x}{6} + \\frac{y}{4} = 1$, i.e. $\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{y}{2} = 2$, i.e. $2x + 3y = 12$.\nStatement II is incorrect: the area is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times 6 \\times 4 = 12$ square units, not 6.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":2,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The line $5x - 7 = 0$ is parallel to the y-axis.\nStatement II: For the line $Ax + By + C = 0$ with $B \\neq 0$, the slope is $-\\frac{A}{B}$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: $5x - 7 = 0$ gives $x = \\frac{7}{5}$. This is a vertical line, so it is parallel to the y-axis. True.\nStatement II: Rewrite $Ax + By + C = 0$ as $y = -\\frac{A}{B}x - \\frac{C}{B}$. So the slope is $-\\frac{A}{B}$. True.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":3,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"What is the slope of a line perpendicular to the line joining $(2, -1)$ and $(5, 3)$?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{4}$","$\\frac{4}{3}$","$-\\frac{3}{4}$","$-\\frac{4}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Slope of the given line is $m_1 = \\frac{3-(-1)}{5-2} = \\frac{4}{3}$.\nFor perpendicular lines, $m_1 m_2 = -1$.\nSo $m_2 = -\\frac{1}{m_1} = -\\frac{3}{4}$.\nOption (1), $\\frac{3}{4}$, is only the reciprocal; the negative sign is missing.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":4,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If a line has inclination $\\theta$ with $90^\\circ < \\theta < 180^\\circ$, then for any two distinct points $(x_1, y_1)$, $(x_2, y_2)$ on it, $y_2 - y_1$ and $x_2 - x_1$ have opposite signs.\nStatement II: The line joining $(a, 0)$ and $(0, b)$, where $a > 0$ and $b > 0$, has an acute inclination.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: for an obtuse $\\theta$, $m = \\tan\\theta < 0$. Since $m = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}$ (and $x_1 \\ne x_2$ for a non-vertical line), the numerator and denominator must have opposite signs. True.\nStatement II: $m = \\frac{b - 0}{0 - a} = -\\frac{b}{a} < 0$ because $a, b > 0$. A negative slope means the inclination is obtuse, not acute. False.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":5,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line makes an angle of $60^\\circ$ with the positive direction of the y-axis, measured anticlockwise. What is the slope of the line?","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","$\\sqrt{3}$","$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","$-\\sqrt{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: The positive y-axis is at $90^\\circ$ from the positive x-axis.\nStep 2: Turning a further $60^\\circ$ anticlockwise gives the inclination $\\theta = 90^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 150^\\circ$.\nStep 3: $m = \\tan 150^\\circ = -\\tan 30^\\circ = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nThe tempting option $\\sqrt{3} = \\tan 60^\\circ$ wrongly treats the angle with the y-axis as the inclination.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":6,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The slope and the y-intercept of the line $4x - 3y + 12 = 0$ are, respectively:","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{4}{3}$ and $4$","$\\frac{4}{3}$ and $4$","$\\frac{4}{3}$ and $-4$","$\\frac{3}{4}$ and $4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Rewrite the equation in the form $y = mx + c$.\n$3y = 4x + 12$, so $y = \\frac{4}{3}x + 4$.\nComparing with $y = mx + c$ gives $m = \\frac{4}{3}$ and $c = 4$.\nOption (1) comes from a sign slip when $y$ is moved across. Option (4) swaps the coefficients.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":7,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The area of the triangle with vertices $(0, 0)$, $(6, 0)$ and $(2, 5)$ is:","image":"","options":["15 sq units","30 sq units","13 sq units","17.5 sq units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Formula: Area $= \\frac{1}{2}|x_1(y_2 - y_3) + x_2(y_3 - y_1) + x_3(y_1 - y_2)|$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{2}|0(0-5) + 6(5-0) + 2(0-0)| = \\frac{1}{2}|30|$\nSo the area = 15 sq units.\nCheck: the base is 6 and the height is 5, so $\\frac{1}{2} \\times 6 \\times 5 = 15$. The value 30 forgets the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":8,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The area of the triangle formed by the lines $y = 2x + 3$, $y = -x + 6$ and the y-axis is $\\frac{3}{2}$ square units.\nReason (R): The area of the triangle formed by the lines $y = m_1x + c_1$, $y = m_2x + c_2$ and $x = 0$ is $\\frac{(c_1 - c_2)^2}{2|m_1 - m_2|}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true. Two vertices lie on the y-axis at $(0, c_1)$ and $(0, c_2)$. The third vertex has $x = \\frac{c_2 - c_1}{m_1 - m_2}$. The area is half the base times this height.\nA: here $c_1 = 3$, $c_2 = 6$, $m_1 = 2$, $m_2 = -1$. The area is $\\frac{9}{2 \\times 3} = \\frac{3}{2}$.\nDirect check: the vertices are $(0, 3)$, $(0, 6)$ and $(1, 5)$. The area is $\\frac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times 1 = \\frac{3}{2}$, so A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":9,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line through P(4, 3) meets the x-axis at A and the y-axis at B such that P is the mid-point of AB. O is the origin. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The equation of the line is $3x + 4y = 24$.\n(ii) The area of triangle OAB is 24 square units.\n(iii) The length of AB is 10 units.\n(iv) The perpendicular distance of O from the line is 5 units.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Since P is the mid-point, A = (8, 0) and B = (0, 6). So the line is $\\frac{x}{8} + \\frac{y}{6} = 1$, i.e. $\\frac{x}{4} + \\frac{y}{3} = 2$, i.e. $3x + 4y = 24$.\n(i) Correct.\n(ii) Correct: area $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8 \\times 6 = 24$.\n(iii) Correct: $AB = \\sqrt{64 + 36} = 10$.\n(iv) Incorrect: $d = \\frac{|0 + 0 - 24|}{\\sqrt{9+16}} = \\frac{24}{5} = 4.8$ units, not 5.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":10,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The line $2x - 3y + 6 = 0$ makes intercepts $-3$ and $2$ on the x-axis and y-axis respectively.\nReason (R): If A, B, C are all non-zero, the line $Ax + By + C = 0$ has x-intercept $-\\frac{C}{A}$ and y-intercept $-\\frac{C}{B}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: Put $y = 0$ to get $x = -\\frac{C}{A}$. Put $x = 0$ to get $y = -\\frac{C}{B}$. Equivalently, $\\frac{x}{-C/A} + \\frac{y}{-C/B} = 1$. So R is true.\nA: Here $A = 2$, $B = -3$, $C = 6$.\nx-intercept $= -\\frac{6}{2} = -3$ and y-intercept $= -\\frac{6}{-3} = 2$. So A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":11,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Treating the line $2x - y + 4 = 0$ as a plane mirror, the image of the point (1, 3) is","image":"","options":["$\\left(-\\frac{7}{5}, \\frac{21}{5}\\right)$","$\\left(\\frac{7}{5}, -\\frac{21}{5}\\right)$","$\\left(\\frac{21}{5}, -\\frac{7}{5}\\right)$","$\\left(-3, 5\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the image be Q(h, k). The mirror line must be the perpendicular bisector of PQ.\nThe mirror has slope 2, so PQ has slope $-\\frac{1}{2}$: $\\frac{k-3}{h-1} = -\\frac{1}{2}$, giving $h + 2k = 7$.\nThe midpoint $\\left(\\frac{h+1}{2}, \\frac{k+3}{2}\\right)$ lies on the line: $(h+1) - \\frac{k+3}{2} + 4 = 0$, giving $2h - k = -7$.\nSolving: $h = -\\frac{7}{5}$, $k = \\frac{21}{5}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":12,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line passes through $(-1, 2)$ and has slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$. How many of the following points lie on this line?\n$(2, 0)$, $(5, -2)$, $(-4, 4)$, $(0, 1)$, $(8, -4)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Find the line: $y - 2 = -\\frac{2}{3}(x + 1)$, so $3y - 6 = -2x - 2$, i.e., $2x + 3y - 4 = 0$.\nCheck each point:\n$(2, 0)$: $4 + 0 - 4 = 0$, so it lies on the line.\n$(5, -2)$: $10 - 6 - 4 = 0$, so it lies on the line.\n$(-4, 4)$: $-8 + 12 - 4 = 0$, so it lies on the line.\n$(0, 1)$: $0 + 3 - 4 = -1 \\neq 0$, so it does not.\n$(8, -4)$: $16 - 12 - 4 = 0$, so it lies on the line.\nSo 4 of the 5 points lie on the line.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":13,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["The slope of the y-axis is zero","The inclination of every line parallel to the x-axis is $0^\\circ$","The slope of every vertical line is 1","The inclination of any line always lies between $0^\\circ$ and $90^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the y-axis has inclination $90^\\circ$, so its slope is not defined.\n(2) Correct: a line parallel to the x-axis has inclination $0^\\circ$, so its slope is $\\tan 0^\\circ = 0$.\n(3) Incorrect: the slope of a vertical line is not defined.\n(4) Incorrect: an inclination can be obtuse, since $0^\\circ \\le \\theta < 180^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":14,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (line through the point $(-2, 3)$) with List-II (equation).\nList-I:\n(a) Parallel to the x-axis\n(b) Parallel to the y-axis\n(c) Passing through the origin\n(d) Inclined at $45^\\circ$ to the positive x-axis\nList-II:\n(i) $x - y + 5 = 0$\n(ii) $y = 3$\n(iii) $3x + 2y = 0$\n(iv) $x = -2$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) A line parallel to the x-axis has constant ordinate, so it is $y = 3$, which is (ii).\n(b) A line parallel to the y-axis has constant abscissa, so it is $x = -2$, which is (iv).\n(c) Through $(0, 0)$ and $(-2, 3)$ the slope is $-\\frac{3}{2}$. So $y = -\\frac{3}{2}x$, i.e., $3x + 2y = 0$, which is (iii).\n(d) The slope is $\\tan 45^\\circ = 1$. So $y - 3 = x + 2$, i.e., $x - y + 5 = 0$, which is (i).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":15,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following lines have an undefined slope?\n(i) the line through $(2, 5)$ and $(2, -1)$\n(ii) the y-axis\n(iii) the line $x = -3$\n(iv) the line through $(1, 1)$ and $(3, 1)$\n(v) a line of inclination $90^\\circ$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Undefined: $x_1 = x_2 = 2$, so the line is vertical.\n(ii) Undefined: the y-axis is vertical.\n(iii) Undefined: $x = -3$ is a vertical line.\n(iv) Defined: $m = \\frac{1-1}{3-1} = 0$, so the line is horizontal.\n(v) Undefined: $\\tan 90^\\circ$ is not defined.\nCount = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":16,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The lines $y = x + 1$, $y = 2x + 3$ and $y = 3x + 5$ are concurrent.\nReason (R): Three lines $y = m_1x + c_1$, $y = m_2x + c_2$, $y = m_3x + c_3$, no two of which are parallel, are concurrent if and only if $m_1(c_2 - c_3) + m_2(c_3 - c_1) + m_3(c_1 - c_2) = 0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: it is the standard condition for three such lines to be concurrent.\nApply it to A: $1(3-5) + 2(5-1) + 3(1-3) = -2 + 8 - 6 = 0$.\nDirect check: $x + 1 = 2x + 3$ gives (−2, −1), and $3(-2) + 5 = -1$, so the third line also passes through this point.\nSo A is true, and R explains it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":17,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A point P on the y-axis is equidistant from the points (5, 2) and (−1, 6). The ordinate of P is","image":"","options":["−1","2","1","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let P = (0, y). Equate the squared distances from P to the two points.\n$(0-5)^2 + (y-2)^2 = (0+1)^2 + (y-6)^2$\nExpanding: $25 + y^2 - 4y + 4 = 1 + y^2 - 12y + 36$, which gives $29 - 4y = 37 - 12y$.\nSo $8y = 8$, i.e. $y = 1$.\nCheck: both squared distances equal 26.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":18,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Line through $(2, 3)$ parallel to the x-axis\n(b) Line with slope 2 and x-intercept 3\n(c) Line with intercepts 2 and $-3$ on the x- and y-axes\n(d) Line through $(1, 2)$ perpendicular to $x + 2y = 0$\nList-II\n(i) $3x - 2y = 6$\n(ii) $y = 2x$\n(iii) $y = 3$\n(iv) $y = 2x - 6$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) A horizontal line through $(2, 3)$ is $y = 3$, so (a) matches (iii).\n(b) Using $y = m(x - d)$: $y = 2(x - 3) = 2x - 6$, so (b) matches (iv).\n(c) Using $\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y}{-3} = 1$ and multiplying by 6: $3x - 2y = 6$, so (c) matches (i).\n(d) The line $x + 2y = 0$ has slope $-\\frac{1}{2}$, so the perpendicular slope is 2. Then $y - 2 = 2(x - 1)$ gives $y = 2x$, so (d) matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":19,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The line through $(2, 3)$ and $(5, 9)$ is parallel to the line through $(-1, 0)$ and $(1, 4)$.\nReason (R): Two non-vertical lines are parallel if and only if their slopes are equal.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Slope of the first line $= \\frac{9-3}{5-2} = 2$.\nSlope of the second line $= \\frac{4-0}{1-(-1)} = 2$.\nThe slopes are equal. The lines are also distinct: on the first line, $x = -1$ gives $y = 3 + 2(-3) = -3 \\neq 0$, so $(-1, 0)$ does not lie on it. Hence A is true.\nR is the NCERT condition for parallel lines, so R is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":20,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The segment of a line between the coordinate axes has end A on the x-axis and end B on the y-axis. The point $P(2, 1)$ divides AB internally so that $AP : PB = 2 : 3$. The y-intercept of the line is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{10}{3}$","$\\frac{5}{2}$","$\\frac{5}{3}$","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Let $A = (a, 0)$ and $B = (0, b)$.\nSection formula with ratio $2 : 3$: $P = \\left(\\frac{2 \\cdot 0 + 3a}{5}, \\frac{2b + 3 \\cdot 0}{5}\\right)$.\nCompare with $P(2, 1)$: $\\frac{3a}{5} = 2$, so $a = \\frac{10}{3}$. Also $\\frac{2b}{5} = 1$, so $b = \\frac{5}{2}$.\nThe line is $\\frac{3x}{10} + \\frac{2y}{5} = 1$, i.e. $3x + 4y = 10$. Check: $3(2) + 4(1) = 10$.\nThe y-intercept is $\\frac{5}{2}$. Reversing the ratio would give $\\frac{5}{3}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":21,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The points on the y-axis whose perpendicular distance from the line $4x - 3y + 6 = 0$ is 3 units are $(0, -3)$ and $(0, 7)$.\nReason (R): For a point $(0, k)$, the condition is $\\frac{|-3k + 6|}{5} = 3$, which gives two values of k.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: For (0, k), $d = \\frac{|4(0) - 3k + 6|}{\\sqrt{16+9}} = \\frac{|6 - 3k|}{5}$. Setting this equal to 3 is the correct condition, so R is true.\nSolving: $6 - 3k = 15$ gives $k = -3$, and $6 - 3k = -15$ gives $k = 7$.\nSo the points are (0, –3) and (0, 7), and A is true.\nA follows directly from solving the equation in R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":22,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Treating the line $x + y - 1 = 0$ as a plane mirror, find the image of the point $P(2, 1)$ in it.","image":"","options":["$(0, -1)$","$(-1, 0)$","$(1, 0)$","$(0, 1)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the image be $Q(h, k)$. The mirror line is the perpendicular bisector of PQ.\nPerpendicularity: the mirror line has slope $-1$, so PQ has slope $1$. Hence $\\frac{k-1}{h-2} = 1$, which gives $h - k = 1$.\nMid-point on the mirror: $\\frac{h+2}{2} + \\frac{k+1}{2} - 1 = 0$, which gives $h + k = -1$.\nSolving the two equations: $h = 0$ and $k = -1$, so $Q = (0, -1)$.\nThe point $(1, 0)$ is only the foot of the perpendicular (the mid-point of PQ), not the image.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":23,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"For what value of $k$ are the three lines $x + y - 5 = 0$, $2x - y - 1 = 0$ and $kx + 3y - 11 = 0$ concurrent?","image":"","options":["$k = 1$","$k = 2$","$k = -1$","$k = 3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Concurrent lines pass through one common point, so the point of intersection of two of the lines must lie on the third.\nAdding $x + y = 5$ and $2x - y = 1$ gives $3x = 6$, so $x = 2$ and $y = 3$.\nSubstitute $(2, 3)$ in the third line: $2k + 9 - 11 = 0$.\nSo $2k = 2$, which gives $k = 1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":24,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A boy stands at the point (1, 2) and wants to reach the straight road $3x + 4y = 20$ by the shortest route. The equation of the path he should follow is","image":"","options":["$3x + 4y - 11 = 0$","$4x - 3y + 2 = 0$","$4x + 3y - 10 = 0$","$3x - 4y + 5 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The shortest route from a point to a line is along the perpendicular to the line.\nThe road has slope $-\\frac{3}{4}$, so the path has slope $\\frac{4}{3}$.\nThrough (1, 2): $y - 2 = \\frac{4}{3}(x - 1)$, which gives $4x - 3y + 2 = 0$.\nOption (1) is parallel to the road, not perpendicular to it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":25,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The line through the points $(1, 2)$ and $(4, k)$ is parallel to the line through $(0, 0)$ and $(3, 6)$. The value of $k$ is:","image":"","options":["6","8","4","10"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the two lines are parallel, so their slopes are equal.\nSlope of the line through $(0,0)$ and $(3,6)$ is $\\frac{6-0}{3-0} = 2$.\nSlope of the line through $(1,2)$ and $(4,k)$ is $\\frac{k-2}{3}$.\nSetting $\\frac{k-2}{3} = 2$ gives $k - 2 = 6$, so $k = 8$.\nOption (1), 6, comes from wrongly taking the slope as $\\frac{k}{3} = 2$ and forgetting to subtract 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":26,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The equation of the line passing through $(2, -3)$ with slope 4 is","image":"","options":["$4x - y - 11 = 0$","$4x - y + 11 = 0$","$4x + y - 5 = 0$","$x - 4y - 14 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Point-slope form: $y - y_0 = m(x - x_0)$.\nSubstituting: $y + 3 = 4(x - 2)$, so $y = 4x - 11$.\nRearranging gives $4x - y - 11 = 0$.\nOption (3) also passes through $(2, -3)$, but its slope is $-4$, so it is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":27,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The point on the y-axis that is equidistant from $(3, 2)$ and $(-1, 6)$ is:","image":"","options":["$(0, 3)$","$(0, -3)$","$(3, 0)$","$(0, 4)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the point be $(0, k)$, since any point on the y-axis has $x = 0$.\nEquate the squared distances: $9 + (k-2)^2 = 1 + (k-6)^2$.\nExpanding: $13 - 4k + k^2 = 37 - 12k + k^2$, so $8k = 24$ and $k = 3$.\nCheck: both squared distances equal 10. The point $(3, 0)$ lies on the x-axis, so it does not qualify.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":28,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Two vertices of an equilateral triangle are $(0, 0)$ and $(2, 2\\sqrt{3})$. If the third vertex lies in the second quadrant, its coordinates are:","image":"","options":["$(-2, 2\\sqrt{3})$","$(4, 0)$","$(-2\\sqrt{3}, 2)$","$(-4, 0)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The side is $\\sqrt{4 + 12} = 4$. Let the third vertex be $(x, y)$.\nThen $x^2 + y^2 = 16$ and $(x-2)^2 + (y - 2\\sqrt{3})^2 = 16$.\nSubtracting the two equations gives $-4x + 4 - 4\\sqrt{3}y + 12 = 0$, so $x = 4 - \\sqrt{3}y$.\nSubstituting: $(4 - \\sqrt{3}y)^2 + y^2 = 16$ gives $4y^2 = 8\\sqrt{3}y$, so $y = 0$ or $y = 2\\sqrt{3}$.\nThis gives the points $(4, 0)$ and $(-2, 2\\sqrt{3})$. Only $(-2, 2\\sqrt{3})$ lies in the second quadrant; $(4, 0)$ lies on the positive x-axis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":29,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the points $(3, -2)$ and $(3, 4)$.\nStatement I: The two-point form $y - y_1 = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}(x - x_1)$ can be used directly to write the equation of the line through these points.\nStatement II: Applying the cross-multiplied form $(y - y_1)(x_2 - x_1) = (y_2 - y_1)(x - x_1)$ to these points gives the equation $x = 3$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is false. Here $x_2 - x_1 = 3 - 3 = 0$, so the fraction $\\frac{6}{0}$ is undefined. The line is vertical and its slope is not defined.\nStatement II is true. Substituting gives $(y + 2)(0) = 6(x - 3)$, i.e., $0 = 6(x - 3)$, so $x = 3$.\nThis matches the fact that both points have abscissa 3, so the line is vertical.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":30,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A ray of light passes through the point $(2, 3)$, strikes the x-axis at a point A and, after reflection, passes through the point $(6, 5)$. What is the x-coordinate of A?","image":"","options":["3.5","4","3","2.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"By the law of reflection, the reflected ray appears to come from the image of $(2, 3)$ in the x-axis, which is $(2, -3)$.\nThe line through $(2, -3)$ and $(6, 5)$ has slope $\\frac{5+3}{6-2} = 2$.\nIts equation is $y + 3 = 2(x - 2)$.\nPutting $y = 0$ gives $2x - 4 = 3$, so $x = 3.5$.\nThe tempting value 4 is only the average of 2 and 6. It ignores the different heights of the two points.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":31,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The distance of the line $2x + y = 15$ from the point P(2, 1), measured along a line making an angle of $45^\\circ$ with the positive x-axis, is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ units","$2\\sqrt{5}$ units","$\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ units","$\\frac{10}{3}$ units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The line through P with slope $\\tan 45^\\circ = 1$ is $y - 1 = x - 2$, i.e. $y = x - 1$.\nFind where it meets $2x + y = 15$: $2x + x - 1 = 15$, so $x = \\frac{16}{3}$ and $y = \\frac{13}{3}$.\nCall this point Q. The required distance is PQ:\n$PQ = \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{10}{3}\\right)^2} = \\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ units.\nOption (2), $2\\sqrt{5}$, is the perpendicular distance, which is not what is asked.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":32,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A ray of light passes through the point (2, 3) and strikes the x-axis at a point A. The reflected ray passes through the point (6, 5). The x-coordinate of A is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{7}{2}$","3","4","$\\frac{5}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"By the law of reflection, the reflected ray appears to come from the image of (2, 3) in the x-axis. That image is (2, −3).\nThe line through (2, −3) and (6, 5) has slope $\\frac{5+3}{6-2} = 2$, so its equation is $y + 3 = 2(x - 2)$.\nPut $y = 0$: $2x - 4 = 3$, so $x = \\frac{7}{2}$.\nCheck: the slopes of A(3.5, 0) to (2, 3) and to (6, 5) are −2 and 2, so the two rays make equal angles with the x-axis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":33,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following sets of three points are collinear?\n(i) $(1, 2), (3, 6), (5, 10)$\n(ii) $(0, 3), (2, 7), (-1, 1)$\n(iii) $(1, -1), (2, 1), (4, 4)$\n(iv) $(-2, 5), (0, 1), (3, -5)$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Compare the slopes of consecutive segments in each set:\n(i) $\\frac{6-2}{3-1} = 2$ and $\\frac{10-6}{5-3} = 2$, so the points are collinear.\n(ii) $\\frac{7-3}{2-0} = 2$ and $\\frac{1-7}{-1-2} = 2$, so the points are collinear.\n(iii) $\\frac{1-(-1)}{2-1} = 2$ but $\\frac{4-1}{4-2} = \\frac{3}{2}$, so the points are not collinear.\n(iv) $\\frac{1-5}{0+2} = -2$ and $\\frac{-5-1}{3-0} = -2$, so the points are collinear.\nThree sets are collinear.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":34,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A point P lies on the same side of the line $3x + 4y - 6 = 0$ as the origin, and also on the same side of the line $5x - 12y + 4 = 0$ as the origin. P moves so that the sum of its perpendicular distances from these two lines is always 2 units. The locus of P is","image":"","options":["$7x + 56y + 16 = 0$","$7x + 56y - 16 = 0$","$16x - 2y - 47 = 0$","$7x - 56y + 16 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Fix the signs using the origin. At (0, 0), $3x + 4y - 6 = -6 < 0$, so the first distance is $\\frac{6 - 3x - 4y}{5}$. Also $5x - 12y + 4 = 4 > 0$, so the second distance is $\\frac{5x - 12y + 4}{13}$.\nCondition: $\\frac{6 - 3x - 4y}{5} + \\frac{5x - 12y + 4}{13} = 2$.\nMultiply by 65: $78 - 39x - 52y + 25x - 60y + 20 = 130$, so $-14x - 112y - 32 = 0$, i.e. $7x + 56y + 16 = 0$.\nCheck with $(0, -\\frac{2}{7})$: the distances are $\\frac{10}{7}$ and $\\frac{4}{7}$, which sum to 2.\nOption (3) comes from ignoring the signs.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":35,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line passes through $(3, -4)$ and makes non-zero intercepts on the coordinate axes that are equal in magnitude but opposite in sign. Its equation is:","image":"","options":["$x + y + 1 = 0$","$x - y + 7 = 0$","$x - y - 7 = 0$","$4x + 3y = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Take the intercepts as $a$ and $-a$.\nIntercept form: $\\frac{x}{a} + \\frac{y}{-a} = 1$, which simplifies to $x - y = a$.\nThe line passes through $(3, -4)$: $3 - (-4) = a$, so $a = 7$.\nThe line is $x - y - 7 = 0$. Its intercepts are 7 and $-7$.\nOption (1) has equal intercepts of the same sign. Option (4) passes through the origin, so both intercepts are zero.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":36,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The equation of the line through $(4, -2)$ parallel to the y-axis is $x = 4$.\nReason (R): The point-slope form $y - y_0 = m(x - x_0)$ gives the equation of every line passing through $(x_0, y_0)$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Every point on a line parallel to the y-axis has the same abscissa. Through $(4, -2)$ that abscissa is 4, so the line is $x = 4$.\nR is false. The point-slope form needs a defined slope $m$, so it applies only to non-vertical lines.\nA vertical line such as $x = 4$ has an undefined slope and cannot be written in this form.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":37,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The point P divides the line segment joining A(2, −1) and B(−4, 5) internally in the ratio 2 : 1. The coordinates of P are","image":"","options":["(0, 1)","(−2, 3)","(−1, 2)","(−3, 4)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: A(2, −1), B(−4, 5), and P divides AB internally with m : n = 2 : 1.\nFormula: $x = \\frac{mx_2 + nx_1}{m+n}$, $y = \\frac{my_2 + ny_1}{m+n}$.\nSubstituting: $x = \\frac{2(-4) + 1(2)}{3} = \\frac{-6}{3} = -2$ and $y = \\frac{2(5) + 1(-1)}{3} = \\frac{9}{3} = 3$.\nThe tempting option (0, 1) results from swapping the roles of m and n. It is actually the point that divides AB in the ratio 1 : 2.\nSo P = (−2, 3).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":38,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"In what ratio does the line $2x + y = 6$ divide the line segment joining $A(1, 1)$ and $B(4, 7)$?","image":"","options":["1 : 3 internally","3 : 1 internally","1 : 2 internally","2 : 3 externally"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the line divide AB in the ratio $k : 1$. The point of division is $\\left(\\frac{4k+1}{k+1}, \\frac{7k+1}{k+1}\\right)$.\nThis point lies on $2x + y = 6$: $2(4k+1) + (7k+1) = 6(k+1)$.\nThis simplifies to $15k + 3 = 6k + 6$, so $9k = 3$ and $k = \\frac{1}{3}$.\nSince $k$ is positive, the division is internal, in the ratio 1 : 3.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":39,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The line passing through the points $(2, 5)$ and $(-1, -4)$ meets the x-axis at the point whose abscissa is","image":"","options":["$-\\frac{1}{3}$","$\\frac{1}{3}$","$3$","$-1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slope: $m = \\frac{-4 - 5}{-1 - 2} = \\frac{-9}{-3} = 3$.\nTwo-point form: $y - 5 = 3(x - 2)$, so $y = 3x - 1$.\nAt the x-axis $y = 0$: $3x - 1 = 0$, which gives $x = \\frac{1}{3}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":40,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The slope of one line is three times the slope of another line. If the tangent of the angle between them is $\\frac{1}{2}$, which of the following can be the slopes of the two lines?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\frac{3}{2}$","$\\frac{1}{3}$ and $1$","$2$ and $6$","$-1$ and $3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Let the slopes be $m$ and $3m$.\nCondition: $\\left|\\frac{3m - m}{1 + 3m^2}\\right| = \\frac{1}{2}$, so $4|m| = 1 + 3m^2$.\nThis gives $3m^2 - 4|m| + 1 = 0$, so $|m| = 1$ or $|m| = \\frac{1}{3}$.\nWith $m = \\frac{1}{3}$ the slopes are $\\frac{1}{3}$ and $1$, which is option (2).\n(1): $m = \\frac{1}{2}$ gives $\\tan = \\frac{4}{7}$. (3): $m = 2$ gives $\\frac{4}{13}$. (4): the ratio is not 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":41,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The lines $y = x + 1$ and $y = 7x - 2$ are equally inclined to the line $y = mx + 5$. The sum of all possible real values of $m$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{2}$","$-\\frac{3}{2}$","$1$","$\\frac{5}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Equal inclination requires $\\left|\\frac{m - 1}{1 + m}\\right| = \\left|\\frac{m - 7}{1 + 7m}\\right|$.\nWith the + sign: $7m^2 - 6m - 1 = m^2 - 6m - 7$, so $6m^2 = -6$, which has no real root.\nWith the − sign: $7m^2 - 6m - 1 = -m^2 + 6m + 7$, so $2m^2 - 3m - 2 = 0$, giving $m = 2$ or $m = -\\frac{1}{2}$.\nCheck: for $m = 2$ both tangents are $\\frac{1}{3}$; for $m = -\\frac{1}{2}$ both are $3$.\nSum $= 2 - \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{3}{2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":42,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line perpendicular to the segment joining A(2, 1) and B(5, 7) divides it internally in the ratio 1 : n (AP : PB = 1 : n). What is the equation of this line?","image":"","options":["$(1+n)(x + 2y) = 4 + 19n$","$(1+n)(2x + y) = 17 + 5n$","$(1+n)(x + 2y) = 19 + 4n$","$2x - y = 3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Dividing point: $P = \\left(\\frac{5 + 2n}{1+n}, \\frac{7 + n}{1+n}\\right)$.\nSlope of AB = $\\frac{7-1}{5-2} = 2$, so the perpendicular line has slope $-\\frac{1}{2}$ and the form $x + 2y = k$.\nSubstitute P: $k = \\frac{5 + 2n + 14 + 2n}{1+n} = \\frac{19 + 4n}{1+n}$, so the line is $(1+n)(x + 2y) = 19 + 4n$.\nCheck n = 1: the line is $x + 2y = 11.5$, and the mid-point (3.5, 4) satisfies it.\nOption (4) is the line AB itself, and option (1) swaps the ratio.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":43,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The points on the y-axis whose perpendicular distance from the line $4x - 3y + 6 = 0$ is 3 units are","image":"","options":["$(0, -3)$ and $(0, 7)$","$(0, 3)$ and $(0, -7)$","$(0, -3)$ only","$(0, 1)$ and $(0, 3)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the point be $(0, k)$.\nDistance condition: $\\frac{|0 - 3k + 6|}{\\sqrt{16+9}} = 3$, so $|6 - 3k| = 15$.\nCase 1: $6 - 3k = 15$ gives $k = -3$.\nCase 2: $6 - 3k = -15$ gives $k = 7$.\nCheck: for $(0, 7)$, $\\frac{|-21+6|}{5} = 3$. Both points work, so option (3) misses the second case.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":44,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The slope of a line parallel to the x-axis is not defined.","A line whose inclination is obtuse has a negative slope.","The slope of the y-axis is not defined.","The slope of the line through (1, 2) and (3, 2) is 0."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: a line parallel to the x-axis has inclination $0^\\circ$, so its slope is $\\tan 0^\\circ = 0$. It is defined.\n(2) Correct: for $90^\\circ < \\theta < 180^\\circ$, $\\tan\\theta < 0$.\n(3) Correct: the y-axis has inclination $90^\\circ$, and $\\tan 90^\\circ$ is not defined.\n(4) Correct: $m = \\frac{2-2}{3-1} = 0$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":45,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The points $(1, 2)$, $(3, 8)$ and $(-1, -4)$ are collinear.\nStatement II: The acute angle between the lines $x - y = 0$ and $x + 2y = 0$ is $\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$.","image":"","options":["Statement I is true, Statement II is false","Statement I is false, Statement II is true","Both statements are true","Both statements are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Slope of the line joining $(1, 2)$ and $(3, 8)$ is $\\frac{6}{2} = 3$. Slope of the line joining $(1, 2)$ and $(-1, -4)$ is $\\frac{-6}{-2} = 3$. The slopes are equal and the lines share a point, so the points are collinear. True.\nStatement II: The slopes are $m_1 = 1$ and $m_2 = -\\frac{1}{2}$. Then $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2} - 1}{1 - \\frac{1}{2}}\\right| = \\left|\\frac{-1.5}{0.5}\\right| = 3$. The acute angle is $\\tan^{-1} 3$, not $\\tan^{-1}\\frac{1}{3}$. False.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":46,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"For the triangle with vertices A(1, 2), B(3, –4) and C(5, 6), match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Median through A\n(b) Right bisector of BC\n(c) Altitude from A\n(d) Side BC\nList-II\n(i) $x + 5y - 11 = 0$\n(ii) $5x - y - 19 = 0$\n(iii) $x + 3y - 7 = 0$\n(iv) $x + 5y - 9 = 0$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Slope of BC = $\\frac{6+4}{5-3} = 5$ and the mid-point of BC = (4, 1).\n(d) Side BC: $y + 4 = 5(x-3)$, i.e. $5x - y - 19 = 0$, which is (ii).\n(a) Median through A joins (1, 2) and (4, 1): slope $-\\frac{1}{3}$, giving $x + 3y - 7 = 0$, which is (iii).\n(b) Right bisector of BC: slope $-\\frac{1}{5}$ through (4, 1), giving $x + 5y - 9 = 0$, which is (iv).\n(c) Altitude from A: slope $-\\frac{1}{5}$ through (1, 2), giving $x + 5y - 11 = 0$, which is (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":47,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A vertical line lies at a distance of 3 units to the left of the y-axis. Which of the following points lies on this line?","image":"","options":["$(3, -5)$","$(5, -3)$","$(0, -3)$","$(-3, 5)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A vertical line at distance $b$ from the y-axis is $x = b$ or $x = -b$.\nIt lies to the left of the y-axis, so its equation is $x = -3$.\nA point lies on this line only if its x-coordinate is $-3$. Only $(-3, 5)$ satisfies this.\n$(3, -5)$ lies on $x = 3$, which is 3 units to the right of the y-axis.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":48,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Two lines pass through the origin. The slope of one is three times the slope of the other, and both slopes are non-zero. The greatest possible value of the acute angle between them is:","image":"","options":["$15^\\circ$","$30^\\circ$","$45^\\circ$","$60^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Let the slopes be $m$ and $3m$.\nThen $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{3m - m}{1 + 3m^2}\\right| = \\frac{2|m|}{1 + 3m^2}$.\nBy AM-GM, $1 + 3m^2 \\ge 2\\sqrt3\\,|m|$, so $\\tan\\theta \\le \\frac{2|m|}{2\\sqrt3\\,|m|} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$.\nEquality holds when $3m^2 = 1$, i.e. $|m| = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$. The slopes are then $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt3}$ and $\\sqrt3$, with inclinations $30^\\circ$ and $60^\\circ$.\nSo the greatest acute angle is $30^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":49,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The tangent of the acute angle between the lines $3x - y + 1 = 0$ and $x - 3y + 2 = 0$ is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{4}$","$\\frac{4}{3}$","$\\frac{8}{3}$","$\\frac{3}{5}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slopes: $m_1 = 3$ and $m_2 = \\frac{1}{3}$.\nFormula: $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{m_2 - m_1}{1 + m_1 m_2}\\right|$.\nSubstituting: $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{\\frac{1}{3} - 3}{1 + 1}\\right| = \\frac{8/3}{2} = \\frac{4}{3}$.\nOption (3) comes from forgetting the denominator, and option (1) is the reciprocal.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":50,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the parallel lines $3x + 4y - 7 = 0$ and $6x + 8y + 11 = 0$.\nStatement I: The distance between them is $\\frac{18}{5}$ units.\nStatement II: To use $d = \\frac{|C_1 - C_2|}{\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}$, both equations must first have the same coefficients of x and y.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement II: The formula applies to $Ax + By + C_1 = 0$ and $Ax + By + C_2 = 0$, so A and B must match. Correct.\nDivide the second line by 2: $3x + 4y + 5.5 = 0$.\nThen $d = \\frac{|-7 - 5.5|}{\\sqrt{9 + 16}} = \\frac{12.5}{5} = 2.5$ units.\nThe value $\\frac{18}{5}$ comes from wrongly using C = 11 without rescaling. Statement I is incorrect.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":51,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Let $L_1: 6x + 8y - 5 = 0$ and $L_2: 3x + 4y + 10 = 0$. Let M be the line midway between them (parallel to both). Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Distance between $L_1$ and $L_2$\n(b) Distance of M from $L_1$\n(c) Distance of the origin from M\n(d) Distance between $y = 3x + 2$ and $y = 3x - 8$\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{3}{4}$\n(ii) $\\frac{5}{4}$\n(iii) $\\frac{5}{2}$\n(iv) $\\sqrt{10}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Rewrite $L_2$ as $6x + 8y + 20 = 0$. Then $d = \\frac{|-5 - 20|}{10} = \\frac{5}{2}$, which matches (iii).\n(b) M is $6x + 8y + 7.5 = 0$, taking C as the average of −5 and 20. It lies half-way, so its distance from $L_1$ is $\\frac{5}{4}$, which matches (ii).\n(c) Write M as $12x + 16y + 15 = 0$. Distance from the origin is $\\frac{15}{20} = \\frac{3}{4}$, which matches (i).\n(d) $d = \\frac{|2 + 8|}{\\sqrt{10}} = \\sqrt{10}$, which matches (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":52,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the line $2x - 3y + 6 = 0$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Its slope is $\\frac{2}{3}$.\n(ii) Its x-intercept is 3.\n(iii) Its y-intercept is 2.\n(iv) In intercept form it is $\\frac{x}{-3} + \\frac{y}{2} = 1$.\n(v) Its inclination is an obtuse angle.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slope-intercept form: $3y = 2x + 6$, so $y = \\frac{2}{3}x + 2$.\n(i) Correct: $m = \\frac{2}{3}$.\n(ii) Incorrect: putting $y = 0$ gives $x = -3$.\n(iii) Correct: $c = 2$.\n(iv) Correct: $2x - 3y = -6$; dividing by $-6$ gives $\\frac{x}{-3} + \\frac{y}{2} = 1$.\n(v) Incorrect: a positive slope means $\\tan\\theta > 0$, so $\\theta$ is acute.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":53,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line meets the x-axis at $(4, 0)$ and the y-axis at $(0, -6)$. Its equation in intercept form is $\\frac{x}{4} + \\frac{y}{-6} = 1$. What is the slope of this line?","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{2}$","$-\\frac{3}{2}$","$\\frac{2}{3}$","$-\\frac{2}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: two points on the line, $(4, 0)$ and $(0, -6)$.\nFormula: $m = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}$.\nSubstituting: $m = \\frac{-6 - 0}{0 - 4} = \\frac{-6}{-4} = \\frac{3}{2}$.\nCheck using intercepts: $m = -\\frac{b}{a} = -\\frac{-6}{4} = \\frac{3}{2}$.\nThe value $-\\frac{3}{2}$ comes from a sign slip, and $\\frac{2}{3}$ inverts the ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":54,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A shopkeeper finds that he sells 900 litres of milk a week at Rs 30 per litre and 1060 litres a week at Rs 32 per litre. Assuming a linear relationship between selling price and weekly sales, how many litres would he sell weekly at Rs 35 per litre?","image":"","options":["1240 litres","1300 litres","1220 litres","1380 litres"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the points (price, sales) = (30, 900) and (32, 1060) lie on a straight line.\nFormula: slope $= \\frac{1060-900}{32-30} = 80$ litres per rupee, so sales $S = 900 + 80(p - 30)$.\nSubstituting $p = 35$: $S = 900 + 80 \\times 5 = 1300$.\nSo the weekly sales = 1300 litres.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":55,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Distance of the point (2, 1) from the line $5x + 12y - 9 = 0$\n(b) Slope of a line perpendicular to the line joining (1, 2) and (4, 8)\n(c) Tangent of the acute angle between $y = x$ and $y = 3x$\n(d) Area of the triangle formed by $x + y = 4$ with the coordinate axes\nList-II:\n(i) 8\n(ii) $\\frac{1}{2}$\n(iii) 1\n(iv) $-\\frac{1}{2}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) $d = \\frac{|10 + 12 - 9|}{\\sqrt{25+144}} = \\frac{13}{13} = 1$. So (a)-(iii).\n(b) The joining line has slope $\\frac{8-2}{4-1} = 2$. A perpendicular slope is $-\\frac{1}{2}$. So (b)-(iv).\n(c) $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{3-1}{1+3}\\right| = \\frac{1}{2}$. So (c)-(ii).\n(d) The intercepts are 4 and 4, so area $= \\frac{1}{2}(4)(4) = 8$. So (d)-(i).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":56,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"In the NCERT derivation of the distance formula, the line $Ax + By + C = 0$ (with A, B, C all non-zero) meets the x-axis at Q and the y-axis at R. The perpendicular PM from a point P is then found using $PM = \\frac{2\\,\\text{area}(\\triangle PQR)}{QR}$. The length QR used in this derivation is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{|C|\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}}{|AB|}$","$\\frac{|C|}{\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}}$","$\\frac{|AB|\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}}{|C|}$","$\\frac{C^2}{|AB|}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: The intercept points are $Q\\left(-\\frac{C}{A}, 0\\right)$ and $R\\left(0, -\\frac{C}{B}\\right)$.\nStep 2: By the distance formula, $QR = \\sqrt{\\frac{C^2}{A^2} + \\frac{C^2}{B^2}} = \\frac{|C|}{|AB|}\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}$.\nStep 3: $\\frac{|C|}{\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}}$ is a tempting choice, but it is the distance of the origin from the line, not the length QR.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":57,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the inclination and slope of a line is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The slope of the x-axis is zero.","The slope of the y-axis is not defined.","A line whose inclination is $135^\\circ$ has slope $1$.","A line parallel to the x-axis has inclination $0^\\circ$."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the x-axis has inclination $0^\\circ$, so its slope is $\\tan 0^\\circ = 0$.\n(2) Correct: the y-axis has inclination $90^\\circ$, and $\\tan 90^\\circ$ is not defined.\n(3) Incorrect: $\\tan 135^\\circ = -\\tan 45^\\circ = -1$, not $1$. An obtuse inclination always gives a negative slope.\n(4) Correct: a line parallel to the x-axis has inclination $0^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":58,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["The equation of the x-axis is $x = 0$","The equation of the y-axis is $x = 0$","The horizontal line 3 units below the x-axis is $x = -3$","The vertical line through $(2, 5)$ has slope 0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: every point on the x-axis has ordinate 0, so the x-axis is $y = 0$.\n(2) Correct: every point on the y-axis has abscissa 0, so the y-axis is $x = 0$.\n(3) Incorrect: a horizontal line 3 units below the x-axis is $y = -3$. The equation $x = -3$ is a vertical line.\n(4) Incorrect: a vertical line has inclination $90^\\circ$, so its slope is not defined. It is not 0.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":59,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Find the acute angle between the lines $y = \\sqrt{3}x + 2$ and $\\sqrt{3}y = x - 5$.","image":"","options":["$15^\\circ$","$30^\\circ$","$45^\\circ$","$60^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slopes: $m_1 = \\sqrt{3}$ and $m_2 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ (from $y = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}} - \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}$).\nFormula: $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{m_2 - m_1}{1 + m_1 m_2}\\right|$.\nSubstituting: $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} - \\sqrt{3}}{1 + 1}\\right| = \\frac{2/\\sqrt{3}}{2} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nSo $\\theta = 30^\\circ$. As a check, the inclinations are $60^\\circ$ and $30^\\circ$, which differ by $30^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":60,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The line through $(-1, 4)$ and $(3, 2)$ is perpendicular to the line through $(2, 5)$ and $(x, 11)$. The value of $x$ is:","image":"","options":["$-1$","$14$","$2$","$5$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Slope of the first line: $m_1 = \\frac{2-4}{3-(-1)} = -\\frac{1}{2}$.\nSlope of the second line: $m_2 = \\frac{11-5}{x-2} = \\frac{6}{x-2}$.\nFor perpendicular lines, $m_1m_2 = -1$, so $-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{6}{x-2} = -1$. This gives $x - 2 = 3$, so $x = 5$.\nOption (1), $x=-1$, gives $m_2 = -2$. That would make the lines neither parallel nor perpendicular; it comes from a sign error.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":61,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line is drawn such that its segment intercepted between the lines $y = x$ and $y = -x$ is bisected at the point $(2, 3)$. Find the area of the triangle formed by this line with the lines $y = x$ and $y = -x$.","image":"","options":["5 sq units","10 sq units","6.5 sq units","2.5 sq units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the line meet $y = x$ at $(a, a)$ and $y = -x$ at $(b, -b)$.\nThe mid-point is $(2, 3)$, so $a + b = 4$ and $a - b = 6$. This gives $a = 5$ and $b = -1$.\nThe ends are $(5, 5)$ and $(-1, 1)$. The line is $2x - 3y + 5 = 0$, which both points satisfy.\nThe triangle has vertices $(0, 0)$, $(5, 5)$ and $(-1, 1)$.\nArea $= \\frac{1}{2}|5(1) - 5(-1)| = \\frac{1}{2}(10) = 5$ sq units.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":62,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The points A$(2, 1)$, B$(5, 3)$ and C$(k, 7)$ form a right-angled triangle. The sum of all real values of $k$ for which this happens is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{7}{3}$","$\\frac{1}{3}$","$-2$","$\\frac{5}{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slope AB = $\\frac{2}{3}$, slope BC = $\\frac{4}{k-5}$, slope AC = $\\frac{6}{k-2}$.\nRight angle at B: $\\frac{4}{k-5} = -\\frac{3}{2}$, so $k-5 = -\\frac{8}{3}$ and $k = \\frac{7}{3}$.\nRight angle at A: $\\frac{6}{k-2} = -\\frac{3}{2}$, so $k-2 = -4$ and $k = -2$.\nRight angle at C: $\\frac{24}{(k-2)(k-5)} = -1$, which gives $k^2-7k+34=0$. Its discriminant is $49-136<0$, so there is no real $k$.\nThe sum of the valid values is $\\frac{7}{3} + (-2) = \\frac{1}{3}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":63,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The lines $y = x + 1$ and $y = 7x - 2$ are equally inclined to the line $y = mx + 4$. The possible values of $m$ are:","image":"","options":["$-2$ or $\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$ or $2$","$2$ or $-\\frac{1}{2}$","$4$ only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The condition is $\\left|\\frac{1-m}{1+m}\\right| = \\left|\\frac{7-m}{1+7m}\\right|$.\nSame sign: $(1-m)(1+7m) = (7-m)(1+m)$ gives $1 - 7m^2 = 7 - m^2$, so $m^2 = -1$. There is no real solution.\nOpposite sign: $(1-m)(1+7m) = -(7-m)(1+m)$ gives $8m^2 - 12m - 8 = 0$, i.e. $2m^2 - 3m - 2 = 0$. So $m = 2$ or $m = -\\frac12$.\nCheck $m = 2$: both tangents equal $\\frac13$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":64,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"If the distance between the parallel lines $4x + 3y - 5 = 0$ and $8x + 6y + \\lambda = 0$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ units, then the possible values of $\\lambda$ are","image":"","options":["$\\lambda = 5$ or $\\lambda = -25$","$\\lambda = 2.5$ or $\\lambda = -12.5$","$\\lambda = 5$ or $\\lambda = 25$","$\\lambda = -5$ or $\\lambda = 25$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First make the coefficients equal: $4x + 3y - 5 = 0$ becomes $8x + 6y - 10 = 0$.\nUsing $d = \\frac{|C_1 - C_2|}{\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}$: $\\frac{|\\lambda + 10|}{\\sqrt{64 + 36}} = \\frac{3}{2}$.\nSo $|\\lambda + 10| = 15$, giving $\\lambda = 5$ or $\\lambda = -25$.\nOption (2) results from skipping the step of equalising coefficients.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":65,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following sets of three points are collinear?\n(i) (1, 2), (3, 6), (5, 10)\n(ii) (0, 0), (2, 3), (4, 5)\n(iii) (–1, 4), (1, 2), (4, –1)\n(iv) (2, –3), (4, 1), (6, 4)\n(v) (a, 0), (0, b), (2a, –b), where $a, b \\neq 0$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Three points are collinear if slope AB = slope BC.\n(i) The slopes are $\\frac{4}{2} = 2$ and $\\frac{4}{2} = 2$. Collinear.\n(ii) The slopes are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{2}{2} = 1$. Not collinear.\n(iii) The slopes are $\\frac{-2}{2} = -1$ and $\\frac{-3}{3} = -1$. Collinear.\n(iv) The slopes are $\\frac{4}{2} = 2$ and $\\frac{3}{2}$. Not collinear.\n(v) The line through the first two points is $\\frac{x}{a}+\\frac{y}{b}=1$. For (2a, –b): $2 - 1 = 1$, so the point satisfies it. Collinear.\nSo 3 sets are collinear.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":66,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the line $(k - 3)x + (k^2 - 4)y + (k^2 - 5k + 4) = 0$. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The line is parallel to the x-axis when $k = 3$","The line is parallel to the y-axis when $k = 2$ or $k = -2$","The line passes through the origin when $k = 1$ or $k = 4$","The line is parallel to the x-axis when $k = 2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Parallel to the x-axis needs the coefficient of x to be 0: $k = 3$. Then $5y - 2 = 0$, a horizontal line. So (1) is correct.\nParallel to the y-axis needs $k^2 - 4 = 0$, i.e. $k = \\pm 2$. The x-coefficient is non-zero for both. So (2) is correct.\nThrough the origin needs $k^2 - 5k + 4 = 0$, i.e. $k = 1$ or $4$. So (3) is correct.\nAt $k = 2$ the line becomes $-x - 2 = 0$, which is vertical, not horizontal. So (4) is incorrect.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":67,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Let p and q be the lengths of the perpendiculars from the origin to the lines $x\\cos\\theta - y\\sin\\theta = k\\cos 2\\theta$ and $x\\sec\\theta + y\\,\\text{cosec}\\,\\theta = k$ respectively. If $k = 4$ and $\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{8}$, then $p^2 + q^2$ equals","image":"","options":["10","12","8","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First line: $p = \\frac{|k\\cos 2\\theta|}{\\sqrt{\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta}} = |k\\cos 2\\theta|$.\nSecond line: $q = \\frac{|k|}{\\sqrt{\\sec^2\\theta + \\text{cosec}^2\\theta}} = \\frac{|k|}{\\frac{1}{|\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta|}} = |k\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta| = \\frac{|k\\sin 2\\theta|}{2}$.\nSubstituting $k = 4$ and $2\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{4}$: $p = 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = 2\\sqrt{2}$ and $q = 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} = \\sqrt{2}$.\nSo $p^2 + q^2 = 8 + 2 = 10$. This is consistent with $p^2 + 4q^2 = k^2 = 16$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":68,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line passes through the point $(4, 3)$ and makes positive intercepts on both axes. The triangle it forms with the axes has area 24 square units. The equation of the line is","image":"","options":["$4x + 3y = 24$","$x + y = 7$","$3x + 4y = 24$","$3x + 2y = 18$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let the line be $\\frac{x}{a} + \\frac{y}{b} = 1$ with $a, b > 0$.\nIt passes through $(4, 3)$: $\\frac{4}{a} + \\frac{3}{b} = 1$, i.e. $4b + 3a = ab$.\nThe area condition gives $\\frac{1}{2}ab = 24$, so $ab = 48$.\nSubstitute $b = \\frac{48}{a}$ into $3a + 4b = 48$: $3a + \\frac{192}{a} = 48$.\nThis gives $a^2 - 16a + 64 = 0$, so $(a - 8)^2 = 0$, $a = 8$ and $b = 6$.\nThe line is $\\frac{x}{8} + \\frac{y}{6} = 1$, i.e. $3x + 4y = 24$.\nCheck the others: $x + y = 7$ gives area 24.5, $3x + 2y = 18$ gives area 27, and $4x + 3y = 24$ does not pass through $(4, 3)$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":69,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The foot of the perpendicular drawn from the point $(2, 3)$ to the line $x + 2y - 3 = 0$ is:","image":"","options":["$(1, 1)$","$(3, 5)$","$\\left(0, \\frac{3}{2}\\right)$","$(3, 0)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: The given line has slope $-\\frac{1}{2}$, so the perpendicular through (2, 3) has slope 2. Its equation is $y - 3 = 2(x - 2)$, i.e. $y = 2x - 1$.\nStep 2: Substitute into the given line: $x + 2(2x - 1) - 3 = 0$, so $5x = 5$ and $x = 1$, $y = 1$.\nStep 3: Check: $1 + 2 - 3 = 0$, so (1, 1) is on the line.\nStep 4: Options (3) and (4) also lie on the line, but the segment from (2, 3) to either of them is not perpendicular to it. Option (2) does not lie on the line at all.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":70,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The vertices of triangle PQR are $P(3, 1)$, $Q(-1, 2)$ and $R(1, 6)$. The equation of the altitude through P is","image":"","options":["$x + 2y - 5 = 0$","$2x - y - 5 = 0$","$x + 2y - 7 = 0$","$x - 2y - 1 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Slope of QR $= \\frac{6 - 2}{1 + 1} = 2$.\nThe altitude through P is perpendicular to QR, so its slope is $-\\frac{1}{2}$.\nPoint-slope form: $y - 1 = -\\frac{1}{2}(x - 3)$, so $2y - 2 = -x + 3$, i.e. $x + 2y - 5 = 0$.\nOption (2) has slope 2: it is parallel to QR, not perpendicular. Option (3) has the right slope but does not pass through P.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":71,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"In deriving the distance of a point $P(x_1, y_1)$ from the line $L: Ax + By + C = 0$ (with $A, B, C \\neq 0$), the line is taken to meet the x-axis at Q and the y-axis at R. The coordinates of Q and R are respectively","image":"","options":["$\\left(-\\frac{C}{A}, 0\\right)$ and $\\left(0, -\\frac{C}{B}\\right)$","$\\left(-\\frac{C}{B}, 0\\right)$ and $\\left(0, -\\frac{C}{A}\\right)$","$\\left(\\frac{C}{A}, 0\\right)$ and $\\left(0, \\frac{C}{B}\\right)$","$\\left(-\\frac{A}{C}, 0\\right)$ and $\\left(0, -\\frac{B}{C}\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"On the x-axis, $y = 0$. Then $Ax + C = 0$ gives $x = -\\frac{C}{A}$, so $Q\\left(-\\frac{C}{A}, 0\\right)$.\nOn the y-axis, $x = 0$. Then $By + C = 0$ gives $y = -\\frac{C}{B}$, so $R\\left(0, -\\frac{C}{B}\\right)$.\nThe derivation then uses $PM = \\frac{2\\,\\text{area}(\\triangle PQR)}{QR}$.\nOption (2) swaps A and B. Option (3) loses the minus sign.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":72,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"For what value of $k$ are the points $(k, 4)$, $(2, -2)$ and $(-1, 1)$ collinear?","image":"","options":["$-4$","$4$","$-2$","$2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For collinear points, the area of the triangle is 0:\n$k(-2 - 1) + 2(1 - 4) + (-1)(4 + 2) = 0$\nThis gives $-3k - 6 - 6 = 0$, so $k = -4$.\nCheck: the line through $(2, -2)$ and $(-1, 1)$ is $y = -x$. The point $(-4, 4)$ satisfies it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":73,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Lines with slopes $2$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}$ are parallel.","The x-axis and y-axis are perpendicular because the product of their slopes is $-1$.","A line with inclination $45^\\circ$ is perpendicular to a line with inclination $135^\\circ$.","Lines with slopes $3$ and $-3$ are perpendicular."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the slopes are unequal, so the lines are not parallel. Their product is $-1$, so they are actually perpendicular.\n(2) Incorrect: the axes are perpendicular, but the slope of the y-axis is not defined. The rule $m_1m_2=-1$ holds only for non-vertical lines.\n(3) Correct: $\\tan 45^\\circ \\cdot \\tan 135^\\circ = 1 \\times (-1) = -1$. Also, the two inclinations differ by $90^\\circ$.\n(4) Incorrect: $3 \\times (-3) = -9 \\neq -1$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":74,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The area of the quadrilateral whose vertices, taken in order, are A$(-3, 2)$, B$(1, -2)$, C$(5, 1)$ and D$(2, 6)$ is:","image":"","options":["32.5 sq units","65 sq units","30 sq units","27.5 sq units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Split the quadrilateral along diagonal AC into triangles ABC and ACD.\nArea of ABC $= \\frac{1}{2}|(-3)(-2-1) + 1(1-2) + 5(2+2)| = \\frac{1}{2}|9 - 1 + 20| = 14$.\nArea of ACD $= \\frac{1}{2}|(-3)(1-6) + 5(6-2) + 2(2-1)| = \\frac{1}{2}|15 + 20 + 2| = 18.5$.\nTotal area $= 14 + 18.5 = 32.5$ sq units. The value 65 is twice the area, from forgetting the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":75,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The foot of the perpendicular drawn from the point $(2, 3)$ to the line $x + y - 1 = 0$ is","image":"","options":["$(0, 1)$","$(1, 0)$","$(-1, 2)$","$\\left(\\frac{1}{2}, \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the foot be $M(h, k)$. PM is perpendicular to the line, which has slope $-1$, so PM has slope 1. Hence $k - 3 = h - 2$, i.e. $k = h + 1$.\nM lies on the line, so $h + k = 1$.\nSolving: $h + h + 1 = 1$ gives $h = 0$ and $k = 1$.\nCheck: PM $= \\sqrt{4+4} = 2\\sqrt{2}$, which equals $\\frac{|2+3-1|}{\\sqrt{2}}$.\nOption (2) lies on the line but is not the foot.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":76,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The lines $x = 2$ and $x = -5$ are parallel, even though the condition $m_1 = m_2$ cannot be used to show it.\nReason (R): The condition that two lines are parallel if and only if their slopes are equal applies only to non-vertical lines.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: Both $x = 2$ and $x = -5$ are vertical lines, so each has inclination $90^\\circ$. Lines with equal inclination are parallel, so the lines are parallel. Their slope $\\tan 90^\\circ$ is not defined, so we cannot compare slopes. A is true.\nR: NCERT states the slope criterion $m_1 = m_2$ for non-vertical lines only. R is true.\nR explains why the slope test fails for these lines even though they are parallel.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":77,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the lines $L_1: 4x - 3y + 8 = 0$ and $L_2: 8x - 6y - 4 = 0$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The distance between $L_1$ and $L_2$ is 1 unit.\n(ii) The line equidistant from $L_1$ and $L_2$ is $4x - 3y + 3 = 0$.\n(iii) The line through $(1, 2)$ parallel to $L_1$ is $4x - 3y + 2 = 0$.\n(iv) The origin lies between $L_1$ and $L_2$.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"First write $L_2$ with the same $x$, $y$ coefficients as $L_1$: dividing by 2 gives $4x - 3y - 2 = 0$.\n(i) Incorrect: $d = \\frac{|8-(-2)|}{\\sqrt{16+9}} = \\frac{10}{5} = 2$ units, not 1.\n(ii) Correct: the equidistant line has $C = \\frac{8+(-2)}{2} = 3$, giving $4x - 3y + 3 = 0$.\n(iii) Correct: using $A(x-x_1) + B(y-y_1) = 0$, we get $4(x-1) - 3(y-2) = 0$, i.e. $4x - 3y + 2 = 0$.\n(iv) Correct: at the origin, $L_1$ gives $+8$ and the normalised $L_2$ gives $-2$. Opposite signs mean the origin lies between the lines.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":78,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line passes through the point $(2, -3)$ and has an inclination of $135^\\circ$ with the positive direction of the x-axis. Its equation is:","image":"","options":["$x + y + 1 = 0$","$x - y - 5 = 0$","$x + y - 1 = 0$","$x - y + 5 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Slope: $m = \\tan 135^\\circ = -1$.\nPoint-slope form: $y - (-3) = -1(x - 2)$, which gives $y + 3 = -x + 2$.\nRearranging: $x + y + 1 = 0$.\nWhy not $x - y - 5 = 0$? That line does pass through $(2, -3)$, but its slope is $+1$, i.e. inclination $45^\\circ$, not $135^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":79,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A point P(x, y) moves so that its perpendicular distances from the lines $3x - 4y + 1 = 0$ and $4x + 3y - 2 = 0$ are always equal.\nStatement I: The locus of P is the pair of straight lines $x + 7y - 3 = 0$ and $7x - y - 1 = 0$.\nStatement II: The point (1, 2) lies on the locus of P.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Equal distances give $\\frac{|3x-4y+1|}{5} = \\frac{|4x+3y-2|}{5}$, i.e. $3x - 4y + 1 = \\pm(4x + 3y - 2)$.\nWith the + sign: $-x - 7y + 3 = 0$, i.e. $x + 7y - 3 = 0$.\nWith the – sign: $7x - y - 1 = 0$.\nSo Statement I is true.\nStatement II: At (1, 2), the distances are $\\frac{|3-8+1|}{5} = \\frac{4}{5}$ and $\\frac{|4+6-2|}{5} = \\frac{8}{5}$. These are unequal, so (1, 2) is not on the locus. False.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":80,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The distance between the parallel lines $5x + 12y - 10 = 0$ and $5x + 12y + 16 = 0$ is","image":"","options":["2 units","$\\frac{6}{13}$ units","$\\frac{26}{5}$ units","$\\frac{1}{2}$ unit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A = 5, B = 12, $C_1 = -10$, $C_2 = 16$. The coefficients of x and y are already identical in both lines.\nFormula: $d = \\frac{|C_1 - C_2|}{\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}$\nSubstituting: $d = \\frac{|-10 - 16|}{\\sqrt{25 + 144}} = \\frac{26}{13}$\nSo d = 2 units. The option $\\frac{6}{13}$ comes from wrongly using $|C_1 + C_2|$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":81,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (form of the line) with List-II (equation).\nList-I:\n(a) Point-slope form\n(b) Two-point form\n(c) Intercept form\n(d) Slope m with x-intercept d\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{x}{a} + \\frac{y}{b} = 1$\n(ii) $y - y_0 = m(x - x_0)$\n(iii) $y = m(x - d)$\n(iv) $y - y_1 = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}(x - x_1)$","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) A line through $(x_0, y_0)$ with slope m is $y - y_0 = m(x - x_0)$. So (a)-(ii).\n(b) A line through two points uses the slope $\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}$. So (b)-(iv).\n(c) Intercepts a and b on the axes give $\\frac{x}{a} + \\frac{y}{b} = 1$. So (c)-(i).\n(d) A line with slope m through $(d, 0)$ is $y = m(x - d)$. So (d)-(iii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":82,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the line passing through the points $(-1, 4)$ and $(3, -4)$. How many of the following points lie on this line?\n$(0, 2)$, $(1, 0)$, $(2, -1)$, $(-2, 6)$, $(5, -7)$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slope: $m = \\frac{-4 - 4}{3 - (-1)} = \\frac{-8}{4} = -2$.\nTwo-point form: $y - 4 = -2(x + 1)$, which simplifies to $y = -2x + 2$.\nCheck each point:\n$(0, 2)$: $y = 2$. Lies on the line.\n$(1, 0)$: $y = 0$. Lies on the line.\n$(2, -1)$: line gives $y = -2$. Not on the line.\n$(-2, 6)$: $y = 6$. Lies on the line.\n$(5, -7)$: line gives $y = -8$. Not on the line.\nSo 3 of the points lie on the line.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":83,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The perpendicular distance of the point $(2, -1)$ from the line $5x + 12y - 24 = 0$ is","image":"","options":["2 units","$\\frac{10}{13}$ units","$\\frac{38}{13}$ units","4 units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 5$, $B = 12$, $C = -24$ and $(x_1, y_1) = (2, -1)$.\nFormula: $d = \\frac{|Ax_1 + By_1 + C|}{\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}}$.\nSubstituting: numerator $= |10 - 12 - 24| = 26$ and denominator $= \\sqrt{25+144} = 13$.\nSo $d = \\frac{26}{13} = 2$ units.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":84,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"What is the distance between the parallel lines $y = 2x + 3$ and $y = 2x - 2$?","image":"","options":["$\\sqrt{5}$ units","5 units","$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}$ units","1 unit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: both lines have the form $y = mx + c$, with $m = 2$, $c_1 = 3$ and $c_2 = -2$.\nFormula: $d = \\frac{|c_1 - c_2|}{\\sqrt{1+m^2}}$.\nSubstituting: $d = \\frac{|3-(-2)|}{\\sqrt{1+4}} = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}$.\nSo $d = \\sqrt{5} \\approx 2.24$ units.\nThe value 5 is the vertical gap between the lines, not the perpendicular distance. The value $\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}$ comes from wrongly using $\\sqrt{1+m}$ in the denominator.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":85,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The acute angle between the lines $y = 2x + 1$ and $y = -3x + 4$ is $45^\\circ$.\nReason (R): When two lines intersect, the obtuse angle between them equals $180^\\circ$ minus the acute angle.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A: The slopes are $2$ and $-3$, so $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{-3 - 2}{1 + (2)(-3)}\\right| = \\left|\\frac{-5}{-5}\\right| = 1$, giving $\\theta = 45^\\circ$. A is true.\nR: Adjacent angles at an intersection are supplementary, so $\\phi = 180^\\circ - \\theta$. R is true.\nHowever, A is proved by the slope formula, not by R.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":86,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The equation of the line passing through $(1, -2)$ and parallel to the line $4x - 3y + 7 = 0$ is","image":"","options":["$4x - 3y - 10 = 0$","$4x - 3y + 10 = 0$","$3x + 4y + 5 = 0$","$4x - 3y - 2 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A line through $(x_1, y_1)$ parallel to $Ax + By + C = 0$ is $A(x - x_1) + B(y - y_1) = 0$.\nSubstituting A = 4, B = −3 and the point (1, −2): $4(x - 1) - 3(y + 2) = 0$.\nExpanding gives $4x - 4 - 3y - 6 = 0$, i.e. $4x - 3y - 10 = 0$.\nCheck: $4(1) - 3(-2) - 10 = 0$. The line $3x + 4y + 5 = 0$ is perpendicular to the given line, not parallel.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":87,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The distance between the points $(-2, 5)$ and $(4, -3)$ is:","image":"","options":["10 units","14 units","6 units","8 units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $(x_1, y_1) = (-2, 5)$ and $(x_2, y_2) = (4, -3)$.\nFormula: $d = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}$\nSubstituting: $d = \\sqrt{(4+2)^2 + (-3-5)^2} = \\sqrt{36 + 64} = \\sqrt{100}$\nSo d = 10 units. Adding the differences directly ($6 + 8 = 14$) is a common mistake.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":88,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The points $(-1, -1)$, $(1, 3)$ and $(4, 8)$ are collinear.\nStatement II: The quadrilateral with vertices A$(0, 0)$, B$(4, 3)$, C$(9, 3)$ and D$(5, 0)$, taken in order, is a rhombus.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: the slopes are $\\frac{3+1}{1+1} = 2$ and $\\frac{8-3}{4-1} = \\frac{5}{3}$. They are unequal, so the points are not collinear. False.\nStatement II, step 1: slope AB = $\\frac{3}{4}$ = slope DC $= \\frac{3}{4}$, and slope BC = 0 = slope AD. Opposite sides are parallel, so ABCD is a parallelogram.\nStep 2: diagonal AC has slope $\\frac{3}{9}=\\frac{1}{3}$ and diagonal BD has slope $\\frac{0-3}{5-4}=-3$. Their product is $-1$, so the diagonals are perpendicular.\nA parallelogram with perpendicular diagonals is a rhombus. True.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":89,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (slopes of two lines) with List-II (acute angle between them):\nList-I\n(A) $1$ and $0$\n(B) $\\sqrt{3}$ and $0$\n(C) $2 + \\sqrt{3}$ and $1$\n(D) $2$ and $-\\frac{1}{2}$\nList-II\n(i) $30^\\circ$\n(ii) $45^\\circ$\n(iii) $60^\\circ$\n(iv) $90^\\circ$","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iii, C-i, D-iv","A-ii, B-i, C-iii, D-iv","A-iii, B-ii, C-i, D-iv","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\tan\\theta = |1 - 0|/1 = 1$, so the angle is $45^\\circ$ (ii).\n(B) $\\tan\\theta = \\sqrt{3}$, so the angle is $60^\\circ$ (iii).\n(C) $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{3}}{3 + \\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{1 + \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}(\\sqrt{3} + 1)} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$, so the angle is $30^\\circ$ (i).\n(D) $m_1 m_2 = -1$, so the lines are perpendicular: $90^\\circ$ (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":90,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"O is the origin and B is the point $(4, 2)$. A line is drawn through B perpendicular to OB. It meets the y-axis at C and the x-axis at D. What is the area of triangle OCD?","image":"","options":["20 sq units","25 sq units","50 sq units","10 sq units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slope of OB $= \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}$. For a perpendicular line, $m_1m_2 = -1$, so the required slope is $-2$.\nPoint-slope form through B: $y - 2 = -2(x - 4)$, which gives $y = -2x + 10$.\nSetting $x = 0$: C is $(0, 10)$. Setting $y = 0$: D is $(5, 0)$.\nTriangle OCD is right-angled at O with legs 10 and 5.\nArea $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 5 = 25$ sq units.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":91,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Using slopes only (without the distance formula or Pythagoras theorem), decide the nature of the triangle with vertices $P(1, 1)$, $Q(4, 3)$ and $R(2, 6)$.","image":"","options":["Right-angled at P","Right-angled at Q","Right-angled at R","Not right-angled"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slope of PQ $= \\frac{3-1}{4-1} = \\frac{2}{3}$.\nSlope of QR $= \\frac{6-3}{2-4} = -\\frac{3}{2}$.\nSlope of PR $= \\frac{6-1}{2-1} = 5$.\nCheck the products: (slope of PQ) × (slope of QR) $= \\frac{2}{3} \\times \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) = -1$, so PQ ⊥ QR.\nThe angle at Q, the common vertex of PQ and QR, is 90°. No other pair of slopes has product $-1$, so the right angle is only at Q.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":92,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The equation of the line with slope $\\frac{3}{2}$ that cuts the x-axis at a distance of 4 units to the left of the origin is:","image":"","options":["$3x - 2y - 12 = 0$","$3x + 2y + 12 = 0$","$2x - 3y + 8 = 0$","$3x - 2y + 12 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $m = \\frac{3}{2}$. The x-intercept is left of the origin, so $d = -4$.\nFormula: $y = m(x - d)$.\nSubstituting: $y = \\frac{3}{2}(x + 4)$, so $2y = 3x + 12$.\nThis gives $3x - 2y + 12 = 0$.\nOption (3) also has x-intercept $-4$, but its slope is $\\frac{2}{3}$. Option (1) has x-intercept $+4$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":93,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The line joining $A(k, -1)$ and $B(3, k)$ has inclination $45^\\circ$. The value of $k$ is:","image":"","options":["$1$","$-1$","$2$","$3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: inclination $45^\\circ$, so $m = \\tan 45^\\circ = 1$.\nFormula: $m = \\frac{k - (-1)}{3 - k} = \\frac{k+1}{3-k}$\nSetting it equal to 1: $k + 1 = 3 - k$, which gives $2k = 2$, so $k = 1$.\nCheck: the points are $(1, -1)$ and $(3, 1)$, and the slope is $\\frac{2}{2} = 1$. $k = 3$ is impossible because it makes the line vertical.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":94,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The acute angle between the lines $y = 2x + 1$ and $y = -3x + 4$ is $45^\\circ$.\nReason (R): If $1 + m_1 m_2 = 0$ for two non-vertical lines with slopes $m_1$ and $m_2$, then the lines are perpendicular.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Checking A: here $m_1 = 2$ and $m_2 = -3$.\n$\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{-3 - 2}{1 + (2)(-3)}\\right| = \\left|\\frac{-5}{-5}\\right| = 1$, so $\\theta = 45^\\circ$. A is true.\nChecking R: $1 + m_1 m_2 = 0$ means $m_1 m_2 = -1$, which is the condition for perpendicular lines. R is true.\nFor these lines, however, $1 + m_1 m_2 = -5 \\neq 0$. R plays no part in finding the $45^\\circ$ angle, so it does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":95,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The intercepts made by the line $4x - 3y + 12 = 0$ on the x-axis and y-axis respectively are","image":"","options":["$-3$ and $4$","$3$ and $-4$","$-4$ and $3$","$4$ and $-3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Write the equation as $4x - 3y = -12$.\nDivide both sides by $-12$: $\\frac{x}{-3} + \\frac{y}{4} = 1$.\nCompare with the intercept form $\\frac{x}{a} + \\frac{y}{b} = 1$: $a = -3$, $b = 4$.\nCheck: putting $y = 0$ gives $x = -3$, and putting $x = 0$ gives $y = 4$.\nOption (2) has the signs reversed.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":96,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The distance of the point $(a, b)$ from the origin is $\\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$.\n(ii) The point $(0, -5)$ lies on the x-axis.\n(iii) If P$(x_1, y)$ and Q$(x_2, y)$ are two points, then PQ $= |x_2 - x_1|$.\n(iv) The mid-point formula is the special case $m = n$ of the internal section formula.\n(v) The points $(0, 0)$, $(1, 1)$ and $(2, 3)$ are collinear.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: use the distance formula with $(0, 0)$.\n(ii) Incorrect: its x-coordinate is zero, so it lies on the y-axis.\n(iii) Correct: PQ is parallel to the x-axis, so $PQ = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2} = |x_2 - x_1|$.\n(iv) Correct: putting $m = n$ gives $\\left(\\frac{x_1 + x_2}{2}, \\frac{y_1 + y_2}{2}\\right)$.\n(v) Incorrect: area $= \\frac{1}{2}|0 + 1(3 - 0) + 2(0 - 1)| = \\frac{1}{2} \\neq 0$.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":97,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following pairs of lines are perpendicular to each other?\n(i) The line through $(0, 0)$ and $(2, 3)$; the line through $(1, 1)$ and $(4, -1)$\n(ii) A line of inclination $30^\\circ$; a line of inclination $120^\\circ$\n(iii) A line of slope $4$; a line of slope $-4$\n(iv) A line parallel to the x-axis; the line through $(2, 5)$ and $(2, -1)$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Perpendicular: the slopes are $\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\frac{-2}{3}$, and their product is $-1$.\n(ii) Perpendicular: $\\tan 30^\\circ \\cdot \\tan 120^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}) = -1$. The inclinations also differ by $90^\\circ$.\n(iii) Not perpendicular: the product of slopes is $-16 \\neq -1$.\n(iv) Perpendicular: both points have $x = 2$, so the second line is vertical, and a vertical line is perpendicular to a horizontal one. The test $m_1m_2 = -1$ cannot be applied here because the vertical line's slope is undefined.\nSo 3 pairs are perpendicular.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":98,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The acute angle between the lines $2x - y + 5 = 0$ and $3x + y - 4 = 0$ is:","image":"","options":["$30^\\circ$","$45^\\circ$","$60^\\circ$","$90^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $2x - y + 5 = 0$ gives $y = 2x + 5$, so $m_1 = 2$. $3x + y - 4 = 0$ gives $y = -3x + 4$, so $m_2 = -3$.\nFormula: $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{m_2 - m_1}{1 + m_1 m_2}\\right|$\nSubstituting: $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{-3 - 2}{1 - 6}\\right| = \\left|\\frac{-5}{-5}\\right| = 1$\nSo the acute angle is $\\theta = 45^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":99,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The distance between the parallel lines $3x + 4y - 7 = 0$ and $6x + 8y + 11 = 0$ is $\\frac{18}{5}$ units.\nStatement II: The perpendicular distance of the origin from the line $3x + 4y - 7 = 0$ is $\\frac{7}{5}$ units.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I: First make the x and y coefficients identical. Doubling the first line gives $6x + 8y - 14 = 0$.\nThen $d = \\frac{|-14 - 11|}{\\sqrt{36+64}} = \\frac{25}{10} = 2.5$ units, not $\\frac{18}{5}$. False. The value $\\frac{18}{5}$ comes from wrongly using $\\frac{|-7-11|}{5}$ without matching coefficients.\nStatement II: $d = \\frac{|0+0-7|}{\\sqrt{9+16}} = \\frac{7}{5}$ units. True.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":100,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The equation of the line lying midway between the parallel lines $2x - y + 3 = 0$ and $2x - y - 7 = 0$ is","image":"","options":["$2x - y - 2 = 0$","$2x - y + 2 = 0$","$2x - y - 5 = 0$","$x + 2y - 2 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The midway line is parallel to both lines, so it has the form $2x - y + C = 0$.\nIt must be equidistant from both lines: $|C - 3| = |C + 7|$. With $C \\ne 3$, this gives $C - 3 = -(C + 7)$, so $C = -2$.\nIn other words, C is the average of 3 and −7.\nThe line is $2x - y - 2 = 0$. The line $x + 2y - 2 = 0$ is not parallel to the given lines.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":101,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The slope of the line passing through the points $(2, -3)$ and $(-4, 9)$ is:","image":"","options":["$-2$","$2$","$-\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{1}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $(x_1, y_1) = (2, -3)$ and $(x_2, y_2) = (-4, 9)$.\nFormula: $m = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}$\nSubstituting: $m = \\frac{9 - (-3)}{-4 - 2} = \\frac{12}{-6}$\nSo $m = -2$. Writing $\\frac{x_2 - x_1}{y_2 - y_1}$ by mistake gives $-\\frac{1}{2}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":102,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The area of the triangle formed by the lines $y = 3x - 2$, $y = -x + 6$ and the y-axis is","image":"","options":["8 sq units","16 sq units","4 sq units","32 sq units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Each line meets the y-axis at $(0, c)$, so two vertices are P(0, −2) and Q(0, 6).\nFor the third vertex, solve $3x - 2 = -x + 6$: this gives $4x = 8$, so $x = 2$ and $y = 4$. The vertex is R(2, 4).\nTake PQ = 8 as the base on the y-axis. The height is the distance of R from the y-axis, which is 2.\nArea $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8 \\times 2 = 8$ sq units.\nCheck with the result $\\frac{(c_1 - c_2)^2}{2|m_1 - m_2|} = \\frac{64}{8} = 8$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":103,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Triangle ABC has vertices A(1, 4), B(–2, 0) and C(4, –2).\nStatement I: The equation of the altitude from A is $3x - y + 1 = 0$.\nStatement II: The length of the altitude from A is $\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{5}$ units.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both statements are true","Both statements are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Slope of BC = $\\frac{-2-0}{4+2} = -\\frac{1}{3}$. Line BC is $y = -\\frac{1}{3}(x+2)$, i.e. $x + 3y + 2 = 0$.\nStep 2: The altitude is perpendicular to BC, so its slope is 3. Through A: $y - 4 = 3(x - 1)$, i.e. $3x - y + 1 = 0$. Statement I is true.\nStep 3: Length = $\\frac{|1 + 12 + 2|}{\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{15}{\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{2}$. Statement II (which gives $\\frac{3\\sqrt{10}}{5}$) is false.\nStep 4: Check: area = 15 and BC = $2\\sqrt{10}$, so altitude = $\\frac{2 \\times 15}{2\\sqrt{10}} = \\frac{15}{\\sqrt{10}}$, which agrees.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":104,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line is drawn so that its segment between the lines $y = 2x$ and $x + y = 10$ is bisected at the point (3, 5). The equation of this line is","image":"","options":["$x - y + 2 = 0$","$x + y - 8 = 0$","$2x - y - 1 = 0$","$x - 2y + 7 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the line meet $y = 2x$ at (a, 2a) and $x + y = 10$ at (b, 10 − b).\nThe midpoint is (3, 5): $a + b = 6$ and $2a + 10 - b = 10$, so $b = 2a$.\nThen $3a = 6$, so $a = 2$ and $b = 4$. The ends are (2, 4) and (4, 6).\nThe slope is $\\frac{6-4}{4-2} = 1$, so the line is $y - 4 = x - 2$, i.e. $x - y + 2 = 0$.\nThe other options also pass through (3, 5), but (3, 5) is not the midpoint of their segments.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":105,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Triangle ABC has vertices A$(1, 2)$, B$(4, 3)$ and C$(2, 9)$. Without using the distance formula, decide which of the following is true.","image":"","options":["The triangle is right-angled at A","The triangle is right-angled at B","The triangle is right-angled at C","The triangle is not right-angled"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Find the slope of each side:\nSlope AB = $\\frac{3-2}{4-1} = \\frac{1}{3}$.\nSlope BC = $\\frac{9-3}{2-4} = -3$.\nSlope AC = $\\frac{9-2}{2-1} = 7$.\nCheck each pair: AB·BC = $\\frac{1}{3}\\times(-3) = -1$, so AB ⊥ BC.\nThe two sides AB and BC meet at B, so the right angle is at B. The other products ($\\frac{7}{3}$ and $-21$) are not $-1$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":106,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line meets the x-axis at A and the y-axis at B. The point R(2, 3) lies on segment AB such that AR : RB = 1 : 2. The equation of the line is","image":"","options":["$3x + y = 9$","$3x + 4y = 18$","$3x + y = 18$","$x + 3y = 11$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let A = (a, 0) and B = (0, b).\nR divides AB internally in the ratio 1 : 2 (m = 1 towards B, n = 2 towards A). The section formula gives $R = \\left(\\frac{1\\cdot 0 + 2a}{3}, \\frac{1\\cdot b + 2\\cdot 0}{3}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{2a}{3}, \\frac{b}{3}\\right)$.\nEquating with (2, 3) gives $a = 3$ and $b = 9$.\nIntercept form: $\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{y}{9} = 1$, i.e. $3x + y = 9$.\nOption (2) also passes through R, but it comes from reversing the ratio (taking AR : RB = 2 : 1).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":107,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"For the line $(k-1)x + (k^2-9)y + (k^2+2k-8) = 0$, match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Line is parallel to the x-axis\n(B) Line is parallel to the y-axis\n(C) Line passes through the origin\nList-II\n(i) $k = 2$ or $k = -4$\n(ii) $k = 1$\n(iii) $k = 3$ or $k = -3$\n(iv) $k = -1$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A line parallel to the x-axis needs the x-coefficient to be zero, so $k = 1$. Then the line is $-8y - 5 = 0$, which is a valid horizontal line. Match (ii).\n(B) A line parallel to the y-axis needs the y-coefficient to be zero: $k^2 = 9$, so $k = \\pm 3$. The x-coefficient is then $2$ or $-4$, both non-zero. Match (iii).\n(C) A line through the origin needs the constant to be zero: $(k+4)(k-2) = 0$, so $k = 2$ or $k = -4$. The x-coefficient stays non-zero for both values. Match (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":108,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The line $5x + 2y - 10 = 0$ makes intercepts 2 and 5 on the x-axis and y-axis, respectively.\nStatement II: The line $4y + 7 = 0$ makes an x-intercept of $-\\frac{7}{4}$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: Write $5x + 2y = 10$ and divide by 10. This gives $\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y}{5} = 1$, so the intercepts are 2 and 5. It is true.\nStatement II: $4y + 7 = 0$ is $y = -\\frac{7}{4}$. This is a horizontal line, parallel to the x-axis, so it never meets the x-axis. Its y-intercept is $-\\frac{7}{4}$, but it has no x-intercept. It is false.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":109,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following points lie on the line $3x - 2y + 6 = 0$?\n(0, 3), (–2, 0), (2, 6), (1, 4), (4, 9)","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Substitute each point into $3x - 2y + 6$:\n(0, 3): $0 - 6 + 6 = 0$. Lies on the line.\n(–2, 0): $-6 - 0 + 6 = 0$. Lies on the line.\n(2, 6): $6 - 12 + 6 = 0$. Lies on the line.\n(1, 4): $3 - 8 + 6 = 1 \\neq 0$. Does not lie on the line.\n(4, 9): $12 - 18 + 6 = 0$. Lies on the line.\nSo 4 of the points lie on the line.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":110,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"For what value of $k$ are the lines $x + y - 5 = 0$, $2x - y - 1 = 0$ and $kx + 3y - 11 = 0$ concurrent?","image":"","options":["1","-1","2","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Concurrent lines pass through one common point, so the intersection of any two must lie on the third.\nAdd $x + y = 5$ and $2x - y = 1$: $3x = 6$, so $x = 2$ and $y = 3$.\nPut (2, 3) into the third line: $2k + 9 - 11 = 0$, so $k = 1$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":111,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Two intersecting non-vertical lines $L_1$ and $L_2$ have slopes $m_1$ and $m_2$. Let $\\theta = \\alpha_2 - \\alpha_1$ and $\\phi = 180^\\circ - \\theta$ be the adjacent angles between them. If $\\frac{m_2 - m_1}{1 + m_1 m_2}$ is positive, then:","image":"","options":["$\\theta$ is acute and $\\phi$ is obtuse","$\\theta$ is obtuse and $\\phi$ is acute","both $\\theta$ and $\\phi$ are acute","the lines are perpendicular"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"We have $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{m_2 - m_1}{1 + m_1 m_2}$. If this is positive, $\\theta$ is acute.\nAlso $\\tan\\phi = -\\tan\\theta$, which is then negative, so $\\phi$ is obtuse.\nOption (2) describes the case where the expression is negative.\nOption (4) needs $1 + m_1 m_2 = 0$, where the expression is undefined.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":112,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following lines make intercepts on the axes whose sum is 5 and whose product is 6?\n(i) $3x + 2y = 6$\n(ii) $2x + 3y - 6 = 0$\n(iii) $3x + 2y + 6 = 0$\n(iv) $x - 6y = 6$\n(v) $\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y}{3} = 2$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) $\\frac{x}{2} + \\frac{y}{3} = 1$: intercepts 2 and 3. Sum 5, product 6. Valid.\n(ii) $\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{y}{2} = 1$: intercepts 3 and 2. Valid.\n(iii) $\\frac{x}{-2} + \\frac{y}{-3} = 1$: sum is $-5$. Not valid.\n(iv) $\\frac{x}{6} + \\frac{y}{-1} = 1$: sum is 5 but product is $-6$. Not valid.\n(v) $\\frac{x}{4} + \\frac{y}{6} = 1$: intercepts 4 and 6. Not valid.\nSo exactly 2 lines qualify.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":113,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The points $A(1, 2)$, $B(3, 8)$ and $C(-1, -4)$ are collinear.\nReason (R): Three points $A$, $B$, $C$ are collinear if and only if slope of AB = slope of BC.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Slope of AB $= \\frac{8-2}{3-1} = 3$.\nSlope of BC $= \\frac{-4-8}{-1-3} = \\frac{-12}{-4} = 3$.\nThe slopes are equal and AB, BC share the point B, so A, B, C are collinear. A is true.\nR is the NCERT condition for collinearity, and it is exactly what proves A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":114,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line has slope $-1$. Its inclination (the angle with the positive direction of the x-axis, measured anticlockwise) is:","image":"","options":["$45^\\circ$","$135^\\circ$","$-45^\\circ$","$225^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $m = \\tan\\theta = -1$, with $0^\\circ \\le \\theta < 180^\\circ$.\nA negative tangent in this range means $\\theta$ is obtuse.\nSo $\\theta = 180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ$.\n$-45^\\circ$ and $225^\\circ$ also have tangent $-1$, but they lie outside the allowed range for an inclination.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":115,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A man stands at the junction of two straight roads $x + y = 4$ and $x - y = 2$. He wants to reach a third road $3x + 4y = 38$ in the least time, walking at constant speed. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) He starts from the point (3, 1).\n(ii) The path he should follow has slope $\\frac{4}{3}$.\n(iii) He meets the third road at the point (6, 5).\n(iv) The length of his path is 25 units.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: adding the two equations gives $2x = 6$, so $x = 3$, $y = 1$.\n(ii) Correct: least time at constant speed means least distance, so he walks perpendicular to the road. The road has slope $-\\frac{3}{4}$, so the path has slope $\\frac{4}{3}$.\n(iii) Correct: the path is $4x - 3y - 9 = 0$. Solving with $3x + 4y = 38$ gives (6, 5).\n(iv) Incorrect: the length is $\\frac{|9 + 4 - 38|}{5} = 5$ units, not 25 (25 is only the numerator).\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":116,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The acute angle between the lines $y = 2x + 5$ and $y = -3x + 1$ is $45^\\circ$.\nReason (R): Two non-vertical lines with slopes $m_1$ and $m_2$ are perpendicular if and only if $m_1 m_2 = -1$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A: The slopes are $m_1 = 2$ and $m_2 = -3$.\n$\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{-3-2}{1+(2)(-3)}\\right| = \\left|\\frac{-5}{-5}\\right| = 1$, so $\\theta = 45^\\circ$. A is true.\nR: This is the NCERT perpendicularity condition. R is true.\nA follows from the angle formula $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{m_2-m_1}{1+m_1m_2}\\right|$, not from the perpendicularity condition. Here $m_1m_2 = -6$, so the lines are not even perpendicular. Hence R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":117,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line passes through the point $(4, -1)$ and cuts off equal non-zero intercepts on the coordinate axes. The area (in square units) of the triangle it forms with the coordinate axes is","image":"","options":["9","3","12.5","4.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: the intercepts are equal, $a = b$, so the line is $\\frac{x}{a} + \\frac{y}{a} = 1$, i.e. $x + y = a$.\nSubstituting $(4, -1)$: $a = 4 - 1 = 3$.\nThe line is $x + y = 3$. Its intercepts are 3 and 3.\nArea $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times 3 = 4.5$ square units.\nThe value 12.5 comes from wrongly taking $a = 4 + 1 = 5$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":118,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Points $A(2, 1)$ and $B(0, 2)$ lie on the same side of the line $x + y = 5$. Treat the line as a plane mirror: a ray from A strikes the line at P and reflects to pass through B. Then $AP + PB$ is minimum. Find the coordinates of P.","image":"","options":["$\\left(\\frac{12}{5}, \\frac{13}{5}\\right)$","$(3, 2)$","$\\left(\\frac{3}{2}, \\frac{7}{2}\\right)$","$\\left(\\frac{8}{3}, \\frac{7}{3}\\right)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1: Find the image $A'$ of A in the line. $A'$ lies on the line through A with slope 1 (perpendicular to slope $-1$), so it has the form $(2 + t, 1 + t)$. The midpoint of $AA'$ must lie on the line: $(2 + \\frac{t}{2}) + (1 + \\frac{t}{2}) = 5$, which gives $t = 2$. So $A' = (4, 3)$.\nStep 2: Since $AP = A'P$, the sum $AP + PB$ is least when $A'$, P and B are collinear. The line $A'B$ has slope $\\frac{1}{4}$, so its equation is $y = 2 + \\frac{x}{4}$.\nStep 3: Intersect with $x + y = 5$: $\\frac{5x}{4} = 3$, so $x = \\frac{12}{5}$ and $y = \\frac{13}{5}$.\nThe point $(3, 2)$ is only the foot of the perpendicular from A, not the reflection point.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":119,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The points $A(1, 2)$, $B(3, 6)$ and $C(k, 12)$ are collinear. What is the value of $k$?","image":"","options":["5","6","7","8"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Three points are collinear if and only if slope of AB = slope of BC.\nSlope of AB $= \\frac{6-2}{3-1} = 2$.\nSlope of BC $= \\frac{12-6}{k-3} = \\frac{6}{k-3}$.\nSetting them equal: $\\frac{6}{k-3} = 2$, so $k - 3 = 3$ and $k = 6$.\nCheck: slope of AC $= \\frac{12-2}{6-1} = 2$, which matches.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":120,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"In triangle ABC with vertices $A(1, 7)$, $B(0, 0)$ and $C(6, 8)$, the length of the altitude drawn from A to BC is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{17}{5}$ units","$\\frac{17}{10}$ units","5 units","3 units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: BC passes through $(0,0)$ and $(6,8)$, so its slope is $\\frac{4}{3}$ and its equation is $4x - 3y = 0$.\nFormula: altitude = distance of A from BC $= \\frac{|4(1) - 3(7)|}{\\sqrt{16+9}}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{17}{5}$.\nCross-check: area $= \\frac{1}{2}|1(0-8) + 0 + 6(7-0)| = 17$ and BC $= 10$, so altitude $= \\frac{2 \\times 17}{10} = 3.4$.\nSo the altitude $= \\frac{17}{5}$ units.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":121,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The points (1, –1), (3, 3) and (–2, –7) are collinear.\nReason (R): The coordinates of (–2, –7) satisfy the equation of the line through (1, –1) and (3, 3).","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Line through (1, –1) and (3, 3): slope $= \\frac{3+1}{3-1} = 2$, so $y + 1 = 2(x-1)$, i.e. $y = 2x - 3$.\nCheck (–2, –7): $2(-2) - 3 = -7$, so the point satisfies the equation and R is true.\nA point lying on the line through the other two makes all three points collinear, so A is true.\nR is exactly why A holds, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":122,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The area (in square units) of the triangle formed by the lines $y = 2x + 3$, $y = -x + 6$ and $x = 0$ is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{3}{2}$","3","$\\frac{9}{2}$","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The lines meet the y-axis ($x = 0$) at P(0, 3) and Q(0, 6), so PQ = 3.\nSolve $2x + 3 = -x + 6$: $x = 1$, $y = 5$. So the third vertex is R(1, 5).\nThe height from R to the y-axis is 1.\nArea $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times 1 = \\frac{3}{2}$.\nThis agrees with $\\frac{(c_1 - c_2)^2}{2|m_1 - m_2|} = \\frac{9}{6}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":123,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (line) with List-II (inclination).\nList-I:\n(A) $y = -x + 4$\n(B) $\\sqrt{3}x - y + 2 = 0$\n(C) $y = 5$\n(D) $x + 2 = 0$\nList-II:\n(i) $0^\\circ$\n(ii) $60^\\circ$\n(iii) $90^\\circ$\n(iv) $135^\\circ$","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Slope is $-1$, so $\\tan\\theta = -1$ and $\\theta = 135^\\circ$ → (iv).\n(B) $y = \\sqrt{3}x + 2$ has slope $\\sqrt{3}$, so $\\theta = 60^\\circ$ → (ii).\n(C) $y = 5$ is a horizontal line, so $\\theta = 0^\\circ$ → (i).\n(D) $x = -2$ is a vertical line, so $\\theta = 90^\\circ$ → (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":124,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The points (2, 3), (4, 7) and (6, k) are collinear. The value of k is","image":"","options":["11","9","10","13"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: A(2, 3), B(4, 7), C(6, k) are collinear.\nCondition: slope of AB = slope of BC.\nSlope of AB $= \\frac{7-3}{4-2} = 2$.\nSlope of BC $= \\frac{k-7}{6-4} = \\frac{k-7}{2}$.\nSetting them equal: $\\frac{k-7}{2} = 2$, so $k = 11$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":125,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line passes through the point $(4, 3)$, and the sum of its intercepts on the x-axis and y-axis is $-1$. The possible equations of the line are:","image":"","options":["$3x - 2y = 6$ and $x - 2y + 2 = 0$","$3x + 2y = 18$ and $x + 2y = 10$","$3x - 2y = 6$ and $x + y = 7$","$2x - 3y = 1$ and $x - 2y + 2 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the intercepts be $a$ and $b$, with $a + b = -1$.\nThe point $(4, 3)$ lies on $\\frac{x}{a} + \\frac{y}{b} = 1$, so $\\frac{4}{a} + \\frac{3}{b} = 1$, i.e. $4b + 3a = ab$.\nPut $b = -1 - a$: $-4 - 4a + 3a = -a - a^2$. This simplifies to $a^2 = 4$, so $a = \\pm 2$.\nFor $a = 2, b = -3$: $\\frac{x}{2} - \\frac{y}{3} = 1$, which is $3x - 2y = 6$.\nFor $a = -2, b = 1$: $-\\frac{x}{2} + y = 1$, which is $x - 2y + 2 = 0$.\nBoth lines satisfy $(4, 3)$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":126,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line cuts off intercepts 3 and 4 on the x-axis and y-axis respectively. The length of the perpendicular drawn from the origin to this line is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{12}{5}$ units","5 units","$\\frac{7}{2}$ units","$\\frac{6}{5}$ units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: a = 3 and b = 4, so the line is $\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{y}{4} = 1$, i.e. $4x + 3y - 12 = 0$.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{p^2} = \\frac{1}{a^2} + \\frac{1}{b^2}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{p^2} = \\frac{1}{9} + \\frac{1}{16} = \\frac{25}{144}$.\nCheck: $\\frac{|-12|}{\\sqrt{16+9}} = \\frac{12}{5}$, which agrees.\nSo $p = \\frac{12}{5}$ units. The value 5 is the length of the intercepted segment, not the perpendicular.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":127,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The distance between the parallel lines $y = 2x + 3$ and $y = 2x - 7$ is","image":"","options":["$2\\sqrt{5}$ units","10 units","$\\sqrt{5}$ units","$\\frac{10}{3}$ units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 2, $c_1 = 3$, $c_2 = -7$.\nFormula: $d = \\frac{|c_1 - c_2|}{\\sqrt{1 + m^2}}$\nSubstituting: $d = \\frac{|3 - (-7)|}{\\sqrt{1 + 4}} = \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{5}}$\nSo $d = 2\\sqrt{5}$ units. The value 10 units is only the vertical gap between the lines, not the perpendicular distance.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":128,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Line L passes through the points $(2, 3)$ and $(4, 0)$. A horizontal line lies 3 units below the x-axis, and a vertical line lies 2 units to the left of the y-axis. Find the area of the triangle formed by L, the horizontal line and the vertical line.","image":"","options":["48 square units","24 square units","96 square units","36 square units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Line L by two-point form: $y - 3 = \\frac{0 - 3}{4 - 2}(x - 2)$, which gives $y = -\\frac{3}{2}x + 6$.\nThe horizontal line is $y = -3$ and the vertical line is $x = -2$. These two lines meet at $(-2, -3)$.\nL meets $y = -3$ where $-3 = -\\frac{3}{2}x + 6$, so $x = 6$, giving $(6, -3)$.\nL meets $x = -2$ where $y = 3 + 6 = 9$, giving $(-2, 9)$.\nThe triangle is right-angled at $(-2, -3)$ with legs $6 - (-2) = 8$ and $9 - (-3) = 12$.\nArea $= \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8 \\times 12 = 48$ square units.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":129,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) Distance of the point (2, 1) from the line $3x + 4y + 5 = 0$\n(b) Distance between the lines $5x - 12y + 3 = 0$ and $5x - 12y - 23 = 0$\n(c) Slope of a line perpendicular to $2x - y + 4 = 0$\n(d) y-intercept of the line $4x - 3y + 12 = 0$\nList-II:\n(i) 2\n(ii) 3\n(iii) 4\n(iv) $-\\frac{1}{2}$","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i","a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv","a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(a) $d = \\frac{|6+4+5|}{\\sqrt{9+16}} = \\frac{15}{5} = 3$, so a-ii.\n(b) $d = \\frac{|3-(-23)|}{\\sqrt{25+144}} = \\frac{26}{13} = 2$, so b-i.\n(c) The given line has slope 2, so a perpendicular line has slope $-\\frac{1}{2}$, so c-iv.\n(d) Rewriting, $3y = 4x + 12$, i.e. $y = \\frac{4}{3}x + 4$, so the y-intercept is 4, so d-iii.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":130,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The distance of the line $2x + y = 9$ from the point (2, 1), measured along a line inclined at $45^\\circ$ to the positive x-axis, is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}$","$\\frac{4}{3}$","$\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}$","$2\\sqrt{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The line through (2, 1) with slope $\\tan 45^\\circ = 1$ is $y = x - 1$.\nSolve with $2x + y = 9$: $3x - 1 = 9$, so $x = \\frac{10}{3}$, $y = \\frac{7}{3}$.\nDistance between (2, 1) and $\\left(\\frac{10}{3}, \\frac{7}{3}\\right)$ is $\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^2} = \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}$.\nOption (3), $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5}}$, is the perpendicular distance, not the distance along the given direction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":131,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A triangle is formed by the lines $y = 2x + 3$, $y = -x + 6$ and $x = 0$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) One vertex of the triangle is (1, 5).\n(ii) The area of the triangle is $\\frac{3}{2}$ square units.\n(iii) The triangle is right-angled.\n(iv) The centroid of the triangle is $\\left(\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{14}{3}\\right)$.\n(v) The tangent of the acute angle between the first two lines is 3.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Vertices: the lines meet x = 0 at (0, 3) and (0, 6). Solving $2x + 3 = -x + 6$ gives x = 1, y = 5. (i) Correct.\n(ii) Area $= \\frac{(c_1-c_2)^2}{2|m_1-m_2|} = \\frac{9}{2 \\times 3} = \\frac{3}{2}$. Correct.\n(iii) The slopes are 2, –1 and undefined. The only finite product is $2 \\times (-1) = -2 \\neq -1$, and the vertical side would need a horizontal partner. Incorrect.\n(iv) Centroid $= \\left(\\frac{0+0+1}{3}, \\frac{3+6+5}{3}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{1}{3}, \\frac{14}{3}\\right)$. Correct.\n(v) $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{-1-2}{1-2}\\right| = 3$. Correct.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":132,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(4, -7)$ and is parallel to the x-axis?","image":"","options":["$y = -7$","$x = 4$","$y = 4$","$x = -7$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: every point on a line parallel to the x-axis has the same ordinate (y-coordinate).\nThe line passes through $(4, -7)$, so every point on it has $y = -7$. Its equation is therefore $y = -7$.\nThe most tempting wrong option is $x = 4$. That is the line through the point parallel to the y-axis, not the x-axis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":133,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Distance between $(1, 1)$ and $(4, 5)$\n(B) Area of the triangle with vertices $(0, 0)$, $(4, 0)$, $(0, 3)$\n(C) x-coordinate of the point dividing the join of $(0, 0)$ and $(9, 0)$ internally in the ratio $1:2$\n(D) y-coordinate of the mid-point of the join of $(2, -3)$ and $(6, 11)$\nList-II\n(I) 3\n(II) 4\n(III) 5\n(IV) 6","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $\\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = 5$, so (III).\n(B) $\\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\times 3 = 6$, so (IV).\n(C) $x = \\frac{1 \\cdot 9 + 2 \\cdot 0}{1+2} = 3$, so (I). Reversing the ratio would give 6.\n(D) $\\frac{-3 + 11}{2} = 4$, so (II).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":134,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line perpendicular to the segment joining A(1, 2) and B(4, 8) divides AB internally in the ratio 1 : 2 (measured from A). What is the y-intercept of this line?","image":"","options":["5","6.25","7.5","0"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Dividing point $P = \\left(\\frac{1\\cdot 4 + 2\\cdot 1}{3}, \\frac{1\\cdot 8 + 2\\cdot 2}{3}\\right) = (2, 4)$.\nSlope of AB = $\\frac{8-2}{4-1} = 2$, so the required line has slope $-\\frac{1}{2}$.\nIts equation is $y - 4 = -\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)$, i.e. $y = -\\frac{x}{2} + 5$.\nSo the y-intercept = 5. (Using the mid-point gives 6.25; reversing the ratio gives 7.5.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":135,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (pair of lines) with List-II (relation between them).\nList-I\n(A) Line through $(1,2),(3,8)$ and line through $(0,0),(-1,-3)$\n(B) Lines with slopes $\\frac{2}{3}$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}$\n(C) Line through $(1,1),(2,3)$ and line through $(3,5),(4,7)$\n(D) Lines with slopes $\\frac{1}{2}$ and $2$\nList-II\n(I) Parallel and distinct\n(II) Perpendicular\n(III) Neither parallel nor perpendicular\n(IV) Coincident","image":"","options":["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The slopes are $\\frac{6}{2}=3$ and $\\frac{-3}{-1}=3$, so they are equal. The second line is $y=3x$, and $(1,2)$ does not lie on it. So the lines are parallel and distinct: I.\n(B) $\\frac{2}{3}\\times(-\\frac{3}{2}) = -1$, so the lines are perpendicular: II.\n(C) Both slopes equal 2. The first line is $y=2x-1$, and $(3,5)$ lies on it. So the lines are coincident: IV.\n(D) The slopes are unequal and their product is $1 \\neq -1$, so the lines are neither: III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":136,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following lines have an obtuse inclination?\n(i) the line through $(2, 5)$ and $(-1, 8)$\n(ii) the line through $(-3, -4)$ and $(1, 2)$\n(iii) the line through $(4, -2)$ and $(4, 7)$\n(iv) the line through $(-5, 1)$ and $(3, 1)$\n(v) the line through $(0, 0)$ and $(-2, 3)$\n(vi) the line through $(a, b)$ and $(-a, -b)$, where $a > 0$ and $b > 0$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An inclination is obtuse exactly when the slope is negative, since $\\tan\\theta < 0$ for $90^\\circ < \\theta < 180^\\circ$.\n(i) $m = \\frac{8-5}{-1-2} = -1$, so it is obtuse ($135^\\circ$).\n(ii) $m = \\frac{6}{4} > 0$, so it is acute.\n(iii) $x_1 = x_2$, so the line is vertical with inclination $90^\\circ$, which is not obtuse.\n(iv) $m = 0$, so the inclination is $0^\\circ$.\n(v) $m = \\frac{3}{-2} < 0$, so it is obtuse.\n(vi) $m = \\frac{-b-b}{-a-a} = \\frac{b}{a} > 0$, so it is acute.\nCount = 2.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":137,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"When the line $3x - 4y = 12$ is written in the intercept form $\\frac{x}{a} + \\frac{y}{b} = 1$, the intercept it makes on the y-axis is","image":"","options":["3","$-3$","4","$-4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Divide both sides by 12: $\\frac{3x}{12} - \\frac{4y}{12} = 1$.\nThis simplifies to $\\frac{x}{4} + \\frac{y}{-3} = 1$.\nSo $a = 4$ and $b = -3$.\nThe y-intercept is $-3$. It is negative because the line meets the negative y-axis. Reading 3 ignores the sign.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":138,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The locus of a point that moves so that it is always equidistant from the lines $x + 2y - 4 = 0$ and $2x + y + 1 = 0$ is","image":"","options":["the pair of lines $x - y + 5 = 0$ and $x + y - 1 = 0$","the pair of lines $x - y - 5 = 0$ and $x + y + 1 = 0$","the pair of lines $x - y + 5 = 0$ and $x + y + 1 = 0$","the single line $x + y - 1 = 0$ only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the point be (h, k). Equal distances give $\\frac{|h + 2k - 4|}{\\sqrt{5}} = \\frac{|2h + k + 1|}{\\sqrt{5}}$, i.e. $|h + 2k - 4| = |2h + k + 1|$.\nCase 1: $h + 2k - 4 = 2h + k + 1$ gives $h - k + 5 = 0$.\nCase 2: $h + 2k - 4 = -(2h + k + 1)$ gives $3h + 3k - 3 = 0$, i.e. $h + k - 1 = 0$.\nBoth cases give valid points, so the locus is both lines (the two angle bisectors).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":139,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Exactly two lines pass through $(1, 2)$ making an angle of $45^\\circ$ with the line $3x - y + 2 = 0$, namely $2x + y - 4 = 0$ and $x - 2y + 3 = 0$.\nReason (R): The condition $1 = \\left|\\frac{m - 3}{1 + 3m}\\right|$ splits into two linear equations in $m$, giving two slopes.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The given line has slope 3.\nCase $+1$: $m - 3 = 1 + 3m$ gives $m = -2$.\nCase $-1$: $m - 3 = -1 - 3m$ gives $m = \\frac12$.\nWith $m = -2$: $y - 2 = -2(x - 1)$, i.e. $2x + y - 4 = 0$.\nWith $m = \\frac12$: $y - 2 = \\frac12(x - 1)$, i.e. $x - 2y + 3 = 0$.\nSo A is true. R is also true, and the two cases of the modulus are exactly why there are two lines.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":140,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (line through the given points) with List-II (slope).\nList-I:\n(a) $(1, 2)$ and $(4, 2)$\n(b) $(0, 0)$ and $(1, \\sqrt{3})$\n(c) $(2, 1)$ and $(1, 2)$\n(d) $(-3, 4)$ and $(-3, 7)$\nList-II:\n(i) $-1$\n(ii) not defined\n(iii) $0$\n(iv) $\\sqrt{3}$","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) $m = \\frac{2-2}{4-1} = 0$, so a matches (iii).\n(b) $m = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{1} = \\sqrt{3}$ (inclination $60^\\circ$), so b matches (iv).\n(c) $m = \\frac{2-1}{1-2} = -1$, so c matches (i).\n(d) $x_1 = x_2 = -3$, so the line is vertical and the slope is not defined; d matches (ii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":141,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The length of the perpendicular from the origin to the line making intercepts 6 and 8 on the x- and y-axes respectively is 4.8 units.\nStatement II: If p is the length of the perpendicular from the origin to a line with intercepts a and b on the axes, then $\\frac{1}{p^2} = \\frac{1}{a^2} + \\frac{1}{b^2}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II: the line is $bx + ay - ab = 0$. Its distance from the origin is $p = \\frac{|ab|}{\\sqrt{a^2+b^2}}$, so $\\frac{1}{p^2} = \\frac{a^2+b^2}{a^2b^2} = \\frac{1}{a^2}+\\frac{1}{b^2}$. It is true.\nStatement I: $\\frac{1}{p^2} = \\frac{1}{36}+\\frac{1}{64} = \\frac{100}{2304}$, so $p = \\frac{48}{10} = 4.8$. It is true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":142,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The angle between two lines is $\\frac{\\pi}{4}$ and the slope of one of them is 2. The slope of the other line is:","image":"","options":["$3$ or $-\\frac{1}{3}$","$-3$ or $\\frac{1}{3}$","$-3$ only","$\\frac{1}{3}$ or $3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Let $m_1 = 2$ and $m_2 = m$. Then $\\tan\\frac{\\pi}{4} = 1 = \\left|\\frac{m - 2}{1 + 2m}\\right|$.\nCase $+1$: $m - 2 = 1 + 2m$, which gives $m = -3$.\nCase $-1$: $m - 2 = -1 - 2m$, so $3m = 1$, which gives $m = \\frac{1}{3}$.\nThe modulus produces two lines, one on each side of the given line. Writing '$-3$ only' ignores the second case.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":143,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the equation of the line passing through the point $(-5, 7)$ and parallel to the x-axis?","image":"","options":["$x = -5$","$y = 7$","$y = -5$","$x = 7$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Every point on a line parallel to the x-axis has the same y-coordinate (ordinate).\nThe line passes through $(-5, 7)$, so every point on it has ordinate 7. Its equation is $y = 7$.\n$x = -5$ is the line through this point parallel to the y-axis, so it is the tempting wrong choice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":144,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The mid-point of the line segment joining $(-3, 7)$ and $(5, -1)$ is $(1, 3)$.\nStatement II: The distance between the points $(0, 0)$ and $(3, 4)$ is 7 units.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I: The mid-point is $\\left(\\frac{-3+5}{2}, \\frac{7-1}{2}\\right) = (1, 3)$. True.\nStatement II: The distance is $\\sqrt{3^2 + 4^2} = \\sqrt{25} = 5$, not 7. False, because 7 is just $3 + 4$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":145,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Find the distance between the parallel lines $6x + 8y - 5 = 0$ and $3x + 4y + 5 = 0$.","image":"","options":["1.5 units","1 unit","2 units","3 units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $6x + 8y - 5 = 0$ and $3x + 4y + 5 = 0$.\nFirst make the coefficients of $x$ and $y$ identical. Doubling the second line gives $6x + 8y + 10 = 0$.\nFormula: $d = \\frac{|C_1 - C_2|}{\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}$.\nSubstituting: $d = \\frac{|-5 - 10|}{\\sqrt{36 + 64}} = \\frac{15}{10}$.\nSo $d$ = 1.5 units.\nUsing $C_2 = 5$ without rescaling gives 1 unit, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":146,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The distance between the parallel lines $3x + 4y - 6 = 0$ and $6x + 8y + 9 = 0$ is","image":"","options":["2.1 units","3 units","1.5 units","0.3 units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $3x + 4y - 6 = 0$ and $6x + 8y + 9 = 0$.\nFirst make the x and y coefficients equal: the second line becomes $3x + 4y + 4.5 = 0$.\nFormula: $d = \\frac{|C_1 - C_2|}{\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}$.\nSubstituting: $d = \\frac{|-6 - 4.5|}{\\sqrt{9 + 16}} = \\frac{10.5}{5}$.\nSo $d = 2.1$ units.\nOption (2) comes from using $C_2 = 9$ without first dividing the second equation by 2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":147,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line makes an angle of $60^\\circ$ with the positive direction of the y-axis, measured clockwise (towards the positive x-axis). The slope of the line is:","image":"","options":["$\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","$\\sqrt{3}$","$-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","$-\\sqrt{3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The positive y-axis makes $90^\\circ$ with the positive x-axis.\nRotating it clockwise by $60^\\circ$ brings it closer to the x-axis, so the inclination is $\\theta = 90^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 30^\\circ$.\nSlope: $m = \\tan 30^\\circ = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nTaking $\\tan 60^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}$ wrongly treats $60^\\circ$ as the angle with the x-axis.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":148,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The line $2x + y = 6$ divides the line segment joining A(1, 1) and B(5, 3) in the ratio","image":"","options":["3 : 7 internally","7 : 3 internally","3 : 7 externally","1 : 2 internally"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Let the point dividing AB in the ratio $\\lambda : 1$ be $\\left(\\frac{5\\lambda+1}{\\lambda+1}, \\frac{3\\lambda+1}{\\lambda+1}\\right)$.\nIt lies on the line: $2(5\\lambda+1) + (3\\lambda+1) = 6(\\lambda+1)$.\nSo $13\\lambda + 3 = 6\\lambda + 6$, giving $\\lambda = \\frac{3}{7}$.\nSince $\\lambda > 0$, the division is internal, in the ratio 3 : 7.\nCheck: the point is (2.2, 1.6), and $4.4 + 1.6 = 6$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":149,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the lines $y = 2x + 1$ and $y = -3x + 5$.\nStatement I: The acute angle between them is $45^\\circ$.\nStatement II: The obtuse angle between them is $120^\\circ$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The slopes are $m_1 = 2$ and $m_2 = -3$.\nFor the acute angle: $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{m_2 - m_1}{1 + m_1 m_2}\\right| = \\left|\\frac{-5}{1 - 6}\\right| = 1$.\nSo $\\theta = 45^\\circ$, and Statement I is correct.\nThe obtuse angle is $180^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 135^\\circ$, not $120^\\circ$. Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":150,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The acute angle between two lines with slopes $2$ and $\\frac{1}{3}$ is:","image":"","options":["$30^\\circ$","$45^\\circ$","$60^\\circ$","$75^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $m_1 = 2$, $m_2 = \\frac{1}{3}$.\nFormula: $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{m_2 - m_1}{1 + m_1 m_2}\\right|$.\nSubstituting: $\\tan\\theta = \\left|\\frac{\\frac{1}{3} - 2}{1 + \\frac{2}{3}}\\right| = \\frac{5/3}{5/3} = 1$.\nSo $\\theta = 45^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":151,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The line $4x - 2y + 6 = 0$ is rewritten in the slope-intercept form $y = mx + c$. What are its slope and y-intercept?","image":"","options":["Slope 2, y-intercept 3","Slope $-2$, y-intercept 3","Slope 2, y-intercept $-3$","Slope 2, y-intercept 6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Make y the subject: $-2y = -4x - 6$.\nDivide by $-2$: $y = 2x + 3$.\nComparing with $y = mx + c$ gives $m = 2$ and $c = 3$.\nThe value 6 is the constant term before dividing by the coefficient of y. It is not the intercept.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":152,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The slope of the line passing through the points (2, 3) and (2, –5) is not defined.\nStatement II: The slope of the x-axis is zero.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Both points have $x = 2$, so the line is vertical. Then $x_2 - x_1 = 0$, and $m = \\frac{-5-3}{0}$ is not defined. True.\nStatement II: The x-axis has inclination $0^\\circ$, so $m = \\tan 0^\\circ = 0$. True.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":153,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line L passes through $(-1, 3)$ and is perpendicular to the line joining $(2, -2)$ and $(6, 4)$. The area (in square units) of the triangle formed by L and the coordinate axes is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{49}{12}$","$\\frac{49}{6}$","$\\frac{49}{24}$","$\\frac{7}{2}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Slope of the joining line: $\\frac{4 - (-2)}{6 - 2} = \\frac{3}{2}$. So L has slope $-\\frac{2}{3}$, since the product of perpendicular slopes is $-1$.\nL: $y - 3 = -\\frac{2}{3}(x + 1)$, which gives $2x + 3y - 7 = 0$.\nIntercepts: putting $y = 0$ gives $x = \\frac{7}{2}$; putting $x = 0$ gives $y = \\frac{7}{3}$.\nArea $= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{3} = \\frac{49}{12}$ square units.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":154,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"What is the equation of the right (perpendicular) bisector of the line segment joining the points (2, 1) and (4, 5)?","image":"","options":["$2x - y - 3 = 0$","$x + 2y - 9 = 0$","$x + 2y - 7 = 0$","$x - 2y + 3 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Mid-point of the segment = $\\left(\\frac{2+4}{2}, \\frac{1+5}{2}\\right) = (3, 3)$.\nSlope of the segment = $\\frac{5-1}{4-2} = 2$, so the slope of the bisector is $-\\frac{1}{2}$.\nPoint-slope form: $y - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}(x-3)$, which gives $2y - 6 = -x + 3$, i.e. $x + 2y - 9 = 0$.\nOption (1), $2x - y - 3 = 0$, has slope 2. It is parallel to the segment, not perpendicular to it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":155,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The points $(1, 2)$, $(3, 6)$ and $(5, 10)$ are collinear.\nReason (R): If the area of the triangle formed by three points is zero, the three points lie on a line.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Area $= \\frac{1}{2}|1(6-10) + 3(10-2) + 5(2-6)| = \\frac{1}{2}|-4 + 24 - 20| = 0$.\nA is true, since zero area means the points are collinear.\nR is true; it is the NCERT remark on collinearity.\nR is exactly the reason A holds, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":156,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Lines are drawn through the point $(2, 3)$ making an angle of $60^\\circ$ with the line $x - \\sqrt{3}y = 0$. Their equations are:","image":"","options":["$x = 2$ and $x + \\sqrt{3}y = 2 + 3\\sqrt{3}$","$y = 3$ and $\\sqrt{3}x + y = 3 + 2\\sqrt{3}$","$x = 2$ and $\\sqrt{3}x - y = 2\\sqrt{3} - 3$","only $x + \\sqrt{3}y = 2 + 3\\sqrt{3}$, since just one line is possible"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The given line has slope $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$, so its inclination is $30^\\circ$.\nUsing $\\sqrt{3} = \\left|\\frac{m - 1/\\sqrt{3}}{1 + m/\\sqrt{3}}\\right|$: the + case gives $m - \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} = \\sqrt{3} + m$, which is impossible. The − case gives $m = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.\nThe 'lost' line has inclination $30^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 90^\\circ$, i.e., it is vertical. The formula cannot detect it, so it must be checked separately.\nSo the lines are $x = 2$, and $y - 3 = -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x - 2)$, i.e., $x + \\sqrt{3}y = 2 + 3\\sqrt{3}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":157,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A shopkeeper finds that he can sell 600 kg of rice per week at Rs 40 per kg and 500 kg per week at Rs 45 per kg. Assume the weekly demand is a linear function of the selling price. How many kilograms per week can he expect to sell at Rs 48 per kg?","image":"","options":["440 kg","460 kg","420 kg","380 kg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the points (price p, demand D) are (40, 600) and (45, 500).\nSlope: $m = \\frac{500-600}{45-40} = -20$ kg per rupee.\nTwo-point form: $D - 600 = -20(p - 40)$, so $D = 1400 - 20p$.\nAt p = 48: $D = 1400 - 960 = 440$ kg.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":158,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the triangle formed by the lines $y = 2x + 3$, $y = -x + 6$ and $x = 0$. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) One vertex of the triangle is (1, 5).\n(ii) The area of the triangle is $\\frac{3}{2}$ square units.\n(iii) The triangle is right-angled.\n(iv) The side lying on $y = -x + 6$ has length $\\sqrt{2}$ units.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Find the vertices. On $x = 0$ the lines give P(0, 3) and Q(0, 6). Solving $2x + 3 = -x + 6$ gives $x = 1$, $y = 5$, so R(1, 5).\n(i) Correct.\n(ii) Base PQ = 3, height = 1, so area $= \\frac{1}{2}(3)(1) = \\frac{3}{2}$. This matches $\\frac{(c_1-c_2)^2}{2|m_1-m_2|} = \\frac{9}{6}$. Correct.\n(iii) The slopes are 2 and –1, with product –2, not –1. Neither line is horizontal, so neither is perpendicular to $x = 0$. Incorrect.\n(iv) QR $= \\sqrt{1^2 + 1^2} = \\sqrt{2}$. Correct.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":159,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Line AB passes through $A(-1, 2)$ and $B(2, 2+\\sqrt{3})$. Line M is perpendicular to AB. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["The inclination of AB is $60^\\circ$","The slope of M is $-\\sqrt{3}$ and its inclination is $120^\\circ$","The slope of M is $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$","M makes an acute angle of $60^\\circ$ with the y-axis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slope of AB $= \\frac{(2+\\sqrt{3}) - 2}{2 - (-1)} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$, so its inclination is $30^\\circ$. (1) is false.\nFor perpendicular lines $m_1 m_2 = -1$, so slope of M $= -\\sqrt{3}$. (3) is false.\n$\\tan\\theta = -\\sqrt{3}$ with $0^\\circ \\le \\theta < 180^\\circ$ gives $\\theta = 120^\\circ$. (2) is true.\nThe acute angle between M and the y-axis is $120^\\circ - 90^\\circ = 30^\\circ$, not $60^\\circ$. (4) is false.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":160,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Consider straight lines passing through the point A(3, 4), and let O be the origin. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Exactly one line through A is at a distance 5 units from O, and it is $3x + 4y = 25$.\n(ii) No line through A is at a distance 6 units from O.\n(iii) Exactly two lines through A are at a distance 3 units from O, and one of them is $x = 3$.\n(iv) The line through A parallel to OA is at a distance 5 units from O.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: the distance from O of any line through A is at most OA = 5. The maximum occurs only for the line perpendicular to OA.\n(i) Correct: the line through A perpendicular to OA has slope $-\\frac{3}{4}$, giving $3x + 4y = 25$. Its distance is $\\frac{25}{5} = 5$.\n(ii) Correct: 6 > 5, so no such line exists.\n(iii) Correct: x = 3 passes through A and is at distance 3 from O. For $y - 4 = m(x - 3)$, the condition $\\frac{|4 - 3m|}{\\sqrt{1+m^2}} = 3$ gives $m = \\frac{7}{24}$, a second line.\n(iv) Incorrect: this line passes through O, so its distance from O is 0.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":161,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following lines are perpendicular to the line $3x - 4y + 7 = 0$?\n$4x + 3y = 1$\n$8x + 6y + 5 = 0$\n$3x + 4y = 2$\n$4x - 3y + 1 = 0$\n$y = -\\frac{4}{3}x + 9$\n$6x - 8y = 3$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Slope of the given line is $\\frac{3}{4}$. A perpendicular line must have slope $-\\frac{4}{3}$, since $m_1 m_2 = -1$.\nSlopes of the six lines:\n$4x + 3y = 1$: $-\\frac{4}{3}$ ✓\n$8x + 6y + 5 = 0$: $-\\frac{4}{3}$ ✓\n$3x + 4y = 2$: $-\\frac{3}{4}$ ✗\n$4x - 3y + 1 = 0$: $\\frac{4}{3}$ ✗\n$y = -\\frac{4}{3}x + 9$: $-\\frac{4}{3}$ ✓\n$6x - 8y = 3$: $\\frac{3}{4}$, which is parallel ✗\nSo 3 lines are perpendicular.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":162,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line through $(2, 1)$ makes positive intercepts $a$ and $b$ on the x-axis and y-axis, respectively. The triangle it forms with the coordinate axes has area 4 square units. The value of $a + b$ is:","image":"","options":["8","5","6","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Area condition: $\\frac{1}{2}ab = 4$, so $ab = 8$.\nThe point lies on the line: $\\frac{2}{a} + \\frac{1}{b} = 1$, so $2b + a = ab = 8$. This gives $a = 8 - 2b$.\nSubstituting: $(8 - 2b)b = 8$, so $2b^2 - 8b + 8 = 0$, i.e. $(b - 2)^2 = 0$.\nSo $b = 2$ and $a = 4$. The line is $x + 2y = 4$.\nTherefore $a + b = 6$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":163,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The distance between the lines $3x + 4y - 5 = 0$ and $6x + 8y + 15 = 0$ is 4 units.\nReason (R): The distance between the parallel lines $Ax + By + C_1 = 0$ and $Ax + By + C_2 = 0$ is $\\frac{|C_1 - C_2|}{\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true. It is the standard formula, valid only when both lines have the same A and B.\nTo use it here, rewrite the first line as $6x + 8y - 10 = 0$.\nThen $d = \\frac{|-10 - 15|}{\\sqrt{36 + 64}} = \\frac{25}{10} = 2.5$ units.\nThe value 4 comes from wrongly using $\\frac{|-5 - 15|}{5}$ without making the coefficients equal. So A is false.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":164,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The slope of the line passing through the points (2, 3) and (2, –5) is not defined.\nStatement II: A line whose inclination is 0° has slope 0.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Both points have $x = 2$, so the line is vertical. Then $m = \\frac{-5-3}{2-2} = \\frac{-8}{0}$, which is not defined. Correct.\nStatement II: $m = \\tan 0^\\circ = 0$. Lines parallel to or along the x-axis have slope zero. Correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":165,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Two points A and B lie on the line $x - y + 1 = 0$, and each is at a perpendicular distance of 2 units from the line $3x + 4y = 0$. The distance AB is","image":"","options":["$\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{7}$ units","$\\frac{10\\sqrt{2}}{7}$ units","$\\frac{20}{7}$ units","4 units"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Take a general point on $x - y + 1 = 0$ as $(t, t+1)$.\nCondition: $\\frac{|3t + 4t + 4|}{5} = 2$, so $|7t + 4| = 10$.\nSolving: $t = \\frac{6}{7}$ or $t = -2$. This gives $A\\left(\\frac{6}{7}, \\frac{13}{7}\\right)$ and $B(-2, -1)$.\nAB $= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{20}{7}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{20}{7}\\right)^2} = \\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{7} \\approx 4.04$ units.\nOption (3) forgets the y-difference. Option (4) wrongly adds the two distances, 2 + 2.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":166,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (form of equation of a line) with List-II (equation).\nList-I:\n(a) Point-slope form\n(b) Two-point form\n(c) Slope-intercept form (y-intercept c)\n(d) Intercept form\nList-II:\n(i) $\\frac{x}{a} + \\frac{y}{b} = 1$\n(ii) $y = mx + c$\n(iii) $y - y_0 = m(x - x_0)$\n(iv) $y - y_1 = \\frac{y_2 - y_1}{x_2 - x_1}(x - x_1)$","image":"","options":["a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-ii, b-iv, c-iii, d-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) A line with slope m through $(x_0, y_0)$ gives $y - y_0 = m(x - x_0)$, so a-iii.\n(b) A line through two points uses the slope $\\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}$, so b-iv.\n(c) A line with slope m and y-intercept c is $y = mx + c$, so c-ii.\n(d) A line with intercepts a and b on the axes is $\\frac{x}{a}+\\frac{y}{b}=1$, so d-i.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":167,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"The point R(2, 3) divides a line segment AB, where A lies on the x-axis and B lies on the y-axis, internally in the ratio AR : RB = 1 : 2. What is the equation of the line AB?","image":"","options":["$3x + 4y = 18$","$3x + 2y = 12$","$3x + y = 9$","$x + 3y = 11$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let A = (a, 0) and B = (0, b). By the section formula with ratio 1 : 2:\n$R = \\left(\\frac{1\\cdot 0 + 2a}{3}, \\frac{1\\cdot b + 2\\cdot 0}{3}\\right) = \\left(\\frac{2a}{3}, \\frac{b}{3}\\right)$.\nSo $\\frac{2a}{3} = 2$ gives a = 3, and $\\frac{b}{3} = 3$ gives b = 9.\nIntercept form: $\\frac{x}{3} + \\frac{y}{9} = 1$, i.e. $3x + y = 9$. Check with R: 6 + 3 = 9.\nOption (1) comes from applying the ratio the wrong way round.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":168,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line passes through $(3, -1)$ and makes an angle of $135^\\circ$ with the positive direction of the x-axis. Its equation is","image":"","options":["$x - y - 4 = 0$","$x + y + 2 = 0$","$x + y - 2 = 0$","$x - y + 4 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Slope: $m = \\tan 135^\\circ = -1$.\nPoint-slope form: $y + 1 = -1(x - 3)$, which gives $y = -x + 2$.\nSo the equation is $x + y - 2 = 0$.\n$x - y - 4 = 0$ also passes through $(3, -1)$, but its slope is $+1$, which corresponds to an inclination of $45^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":169,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line has slope $-3$ and cuts the x-axis at 2 units to the right of the origin. Its equation is","image":"","options":["$3x + y + 6 = 0$","$x + 3y - 2 = 0$","$3x + y - 6 = 0$","$3x - y - 6 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $m = -3$ and x-intercept $d = 2$.\nFormula: $y = m(x - d)$.\nSubstituting: $y = -3(x - 2) = -3x + 6$.\nSo the equation is $3x + y - 6 = 0$.\nCheck: putting $y = 0$ gives $x = 2$. Option (1) would give the x-intercept $-2$ instead.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":170,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The lines $2x + 3y - 5 = 0$ and $4x + 6y + 7 = 0$ are parallel.\nReason (R): Two non-vertical lines are parallel if and only if their slopes are equal.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Slope of $2x + 3y - 5 = 0$ is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\nSlope of $4x + 6y + 7 = 0$ is $-\\frac{4}{6} = -\\frac{2}{3}$.\nThe slopes are equal, so the lines are parallel. A is true.\nR is the NCERT condition for parallel lines, and it is exactly what proves A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":171,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The distance of the point $(1, 2)$ from the line $3x + 4y - 1 = 0$ is the same as its distance from the line $6x + 8y - 2 = 0$.\nReason (R): If the equation $Ax + By + C = 0$ is multiplied by a non-zero constant k, both $|Ax_1 + By_1 + C|$ and $\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}$ get multiplied by $|k|$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: For the first line, $d = \\frac{|3+8-1|}{5} = 2$.\nFor the second line, $d = \\frac{|6+16-2|}{10} = 2$. The distances are equal, so A is true.\nR: The numerator becomes $|k|\\,|Ax_1+By_1+C|$ and the denominator becomes $\\sqrt{k^2(A^2+B^2)} = |k|\\sqrt{A^2+B^2}$. So R is true.\nThe factor $|k|$ cancels, which is exactly why the distances in A are equal. R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":172,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following lines are parallel to $3x - 4y + 1 = 0$ AND at a distance of 2 units from it?\n(i) $3x - 4y + 11 = 0$\n(ii) $3x - 4y - 9 = 0$\n(iii) $6x - 8y + 22 = 0$\n(iv) $3x + 4y + 11 = 0$\n(v) $6x - 8y - 9 = 0$","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let the required line be $3x - 4y + C = 0$. Then $\\frac{|C - 1|}{5} = 2$, so C = 11 or C = −9.\n(i) Correct: C = 11.\n(ii) Correct: C = −9.\n(iii) Correct: dividing by 2 gives $3x - 4y + 11 = 0$.\n(iv) Wrong: its slope is $-\\frac{3}{4}$, so it is not parallel.\n(v) Wrong: it reduces to $3x - 4y - 4.5 = 0$, at distance $\\frac{5.5}{5} = 1.1$.\nThree lines qualify.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":173,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The locus of a point that is equidistant from the lines $3x + 4y - 5 = 0$ and $4x - 3y + 2 = 0$ is a pair of mutually perpendicular straight lines.\nReason (R): The locus of a point equidistant from two intersecting lines is always a single straight line.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Let (h, k) be the moving point. Both lines have $\\sqrt{A^2+B^2} = 5$, so equal distances give $|3h + 4k - 5| = |4h - 3k + 2|$.\nCase +: $3h + 4k - 5 = 4h - 3k + 2$, which gives $h - 7k + 7 = 0$ with slope $\\frac{1}{7}$.\nCase –: $3h + 4k - 5 = -(4h - 3k + 2)$, which gives $7h + k - 3 = 0$ with slope –7.\nThe product of the slopes is $\\frac{1}{7} \\times (-7) = -1$, so the two lines are perpendicular. A is true.\nR is false: the absolute value always produces two cases, giving two lines.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT-MATH-STRAIGHT-LINES-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Straight Lines","qno":174,"subject":"Maths","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Straight Lines","type":"mcq","question":"A line passes through $(1, -1)$ and makes an angle of $45^\\circ$ with the line $2x + y - 7 = 0$. Its possible equation(s) is/are:","image":"","options":["$3x - y - 4 = 0$ or $x + 3y + 2 = 0$","$x - 3y - 4 = 0$ or $3x + y - 2 = 0$","$3x - y - 4 = 0$ only","$x + 3y + 2 = 0$ or $3x + y - 2 = 0$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The given line has slope $-2$. Let the required slope be $m$.\nCondition: $1 = \\left|\\frac{m + 2}{1 - 2m}\\right|$.\nCase 1: $m + 2 = 1 - 2m$ gives $m = -\\frac{1}{3}$.\nCase 2: $m + 2 = -(1 - 2m)$ gives $m = 3$.\nThrough $(1, -1)$: $y + 1 = 3(x - 1)$ gives $3x - y - 4 = 0$, and $y + 1 = -\\frac{1}{3}(x - 1)$ gives $x + 3y + 2 = 0$.\nTwo lines exist, so 'only' (option 3) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Mathematics Class 11, Ch 9 · Straight Lines","difficulty":"medium"}]});
